window.CNPE_DRILL = [
{"id": "1.1#a-service-resolves-in-dns-but-every-connection-i", "sec": "1.1", "q": "A Service resolves in DNS but every connection is refused. Name the three things you check, in order.", "a": "<p>Selector against actual pod labels; pod readiness (an unready pod is not in the slice); the EndpointSlice itself. If the slice has addresses with <code>ready: false<\/code>, it is a probe problem, not a Service problem. If the slice is empty, it is a selector or a scheduling problem.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#why-does-a-networkpolicy-ipblock-naming-a-servic", "sec": "1.1", "q": "Why does a NetworkPolicy <code>ipBlock<\/code> naming a Service's ClusterIP never work?", "a": "<p>Because DNAT happens before policy evaluation: by the time the packet is checked, its destination is a pod IP. Policy is expressed in pod selectors and identities, not virtual IPs. For the API server specifically, Cilium's <code>toEntities: [kube-apiserver]<\/code> is the expressible form.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#your-backend-needs-the-real-client-ip-what-do-yo", "sec": "1.1", "q": "Your backend needs the real client IP. What do you change, and what breaks?", "a": "<p><code>externalTrafficPolicy: Local<\/code> on the Service. The trade: nodes with no backing pod stop serving that traffic, so you now depend on the load balancer's health checks to avoid them, and you lose the even spread that <code>Cluster<\/code> gave you.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#what-is-the-difference-between-a-headless-servic", "sec": "1.1", "q": "What is the difference between a headless Service and a ClusterIP Service with one endpoint?", "a": "<p>Headless has no VIP and no datapath translation: DNS returns pod IPs directly and the client chooses. A one-endpoint ClusterIP still goes through the VIP and its rules, so the client never learns the pod IP. StatefulSets need the former for stable per-pod names (<code>pod-0.svc…<\/code>).<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#team-a-can-curl-team-b-s-service-after-you-added", "sec": "1.1", "q": "Team-a can curl team-b's Service after you added an egress allow, but it still fails. What did you forget?", "a": "<p>Team-b's ingress side. Both ends must open; policies are additive allow-lists evaluated independently at each pod. Also check DNS: if egress is now restricted in team-a, resolving <code>web.team-b.svc<\/code> needs its own port-53 allow.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#an-httproute-applies-cleanly-but-nothing-routes-", "sec": "1.1", "q": "An HTTPRoute applies cleanly but nothing routes. Where do you look first?", "a": "<p><code>status.parents[].conditions<\/code> on the route: <em>Accepted<\/em> false usually means attachment was refused by the Gateway's <code>allowedRoutes<\/code>; <em>ResolvedRefs<\/em> false means a backend Service or a TLS secret is missing (or needs a ReferenceGrant across namespaces). On the Gateway, <em>Programmed<\/em> false means no data plane, which is the permanent state on this lab's main cluster.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#an-httproute-in-team-a-references-a-service-in-t", "sec": "1.1", "q": "An HTTPRoute in team-a references a Service in team-b. Which object, in which namespace, and what does status say until it exists?", "a": "<p>A ReferenceGrant in team-b (the namespace being referenced) with <code>from<\/code> group gateway.networking.k8s.io kind HTTPRoute namespace team-a and <code>to<\/code> kind Service. Until it exists the route reports <code>ResolvedRefs: False<\/code> with reason <code>RefNotPermitted<\/code>, and the implementation answers 500 for that backend.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#two-backendrefs-carry-weight-3-and-weight-1-what", "sec": "1.1", "q": "Two backendRefs carry weight 3 and weight 1. What split is that, and what happens if one backend Service is missing?", "a": "<p>75/25: each weight is a share of the total, so 3 and 1 make four shares. A missing backend produces <code>ResolvedRefs: False<\/code>, <code>BackendNotFound<\/code>; the implementation must still route the valid share and return 500 for the share that points at the invalid backend, so users see intermittent errors proportional to the weight.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#a-stub-domain-for-corp-example-must-resolve-via-", "sec": "1.1", "q": "A stub domain for corp.example must resolve via 10.0.0.53. Where does the change go and how long before it applies?", "a": "<p>A second server block in the <code>coredns<\/code> ConfigMap in kube-system: <code>corp.example:53 { errors; cache 30; forward . 10.0.0.53 }<\/code>. The <code>reload<\/code> plugin applies it within about two minutes without restarting pods. Verify from a pod with nslookup, and check <code>kubectl -n kube-system logs deploy/coredns<\/code> for a Corefile parse error if nothing changes.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#which-kube-proxy-mode-is-ga-and-newest-and-what-", "sec": "1.1", "q": "Which kube-proxy mode is GA and newest, and what changes for NodePort when you switch to it?", "a": "<p>nftables, GA since Kubernetes 1.33 (kernel 5.13+). NodePorts default to <code>--nodeport-addresses primary<\/code>, so they answer on the node's primary IP only; other interfaces and localhost stop answering, and kube-proxy no longer punches firewall holes for the NodePort range. IPVS is deprecated since 1.35; iptables remains the default.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#a-node-shows-25-cpu-usage-and-refuses-to-schedul", "sec": "1.2", "q": "A node shows 25% CPU usage and refuses to schedule a pod requesting 200m. Explain, in one sentence.", "a": "<p>The scheduler counts <em>requests<\/em> against <em>allocatable<\/em>, not usage: the node's requests are already at allocatable even though the processes are idle. Fix by right-sizing the existing requests (or adding capacity), not by adding CPU headroom that already exists.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#which-pod-does-the-kubelet-evict-first-under-mem", "sec": "1.2", "q": "Which pod does the kubelet evict first under memory pressure, and why?", "a": "<p>BestEffort, because it declared no memory request at all and therefore ranks worst. Then Burstable pods exceeding their requests, ordered by how far over they are. Guaranteed last. The kernel's <code>oom_score_adj<\/code> mirrors that ranking so a system-level OOM picks the same victim.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#your-service-has-p99-latency-spikes-every-few-se", "sec": "1.2", "q": "Your service has p99 latency spikes every few seconds; CPU usage sits at 60% of the limit. First metric you check?", "a": "<p><code>rate(container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total[5m])<\/code>. Average usage below the limit hides per-period throttling: the container burns its 100ms quota early and stalls until the next period. Average utilization is the wrong lens for CPU limits.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#why-can-a-vpa-in-auto-mode-and-an-hpa-on-cpu-not", "sec": "1.2", "q": "Why can a VPA in <code>Auto<\/code> mode and an HPA on CPU not coexist on the same workload?", "a": "<p>They form a loop: the HPA scales replicas based on usage-against-requests while the VPA rewrites those same requests, so each one keeps invalidating the other's denominator. Standard resolution: HPA on CPU with VPA in <code>Off<\/code> (advisory), or HPA on a custom/external metric while VPA owns the resources.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#a-drain-hangs-forever-on-one-node-what-are-the-t", "sec": "1.2", "q": "A drain hangs forever on one node. What are the two usual causes?", "a": "<p>A PodDisruptionBudget that cannot be satisfied (often <code>minAvailable<\/code> equal to the replica count), or unmanaged pods: bare pods with no controller, which <code>kubectl drain<\/code> refuses to evict without <code>--force<\/code>. Both are voluntary-disruption mechanics; neither has anything to do with node health.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#is-in-place-pod-resize-usable-on-a-kubernetes-1-", "sec": "1.2", "q": "Is in-place pod resize usable on a Kubernetes 1.36 cluster, and what does the request look like?", "a": "<p>Yes: <code>InPlacePodVerticalScaling<\/code> has been GA since 1.35 (beta and default-on since 1.33). You patch the <code>resize<\/code> subresource (<code>kubectl patch pod x --subresource resize ...<\/code>); a plain spec patch is rejected as immutable. Watch <code>PodResizePending<\/code> (Infeasible or Deferred) and <code>PodResizeInProgress<\/code> conditions, and remember the QoS class cannot change.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#karpenter-refuses-to-consolidate-a-node-you-cons", "sec": "1.2", "q": "Karpenter refuses to consolidate a node you consider empty enough. Where is the reason, and name two things it could be.", "a": "<p>Events on the node with reason <code>Unconsolidatable<\/code>. Typical messages: a PDB prevents pod evictions, a pod carries <code>karpenter.sh/do-not-disrupt<\/code>, no cheaper replacement exists, or the NodePool's disruption budget is exhausted. Cluster Autoscaler's equivalents are the safe-to-evict annotation, uncontrolled pods and local storage.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#a-pdb-has-minavailable-2-for-a-2-replica-deploym", "sec": "1.2", "q": "A PDB has minAvailable 2 for a 2-replica Deployment whose pods are CrashLoopBackOff. Drain hangs. Fix without deleting the PDB?", "a": "<p>Set <code>unhealthyPodEvictionPolicy: AlwaysAllow<\/code> on the PDB: the default <code>IfHealthyBudget<\/code> refuses to evict unhealthy pods until the budget is met, which can never happen here. Alternatively scale the Deployment so the budget is satisfiable. Deleting the PDB removes the protection permanently rather than for the drain.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#what-is-allocatable-and-which-four-kubelet-setti", "sec": "1.2", "q": "What is allocatable, and which four kubelet settings subtract from capacity?", "a": "<p>Allocatable is what the scheduler may hand to pods: capacity minus <code>kubeReserved<\/code>, <code>systemReserved<\/code> and the <code>evictionHard<\/code> thresholds (defaults memory.available 100Mi, nodefs.available 10%, nodefs.inodesFree 5%, imagefs.available 15%). <code>enforceNodeAllocatable<\/code> decides which of those cgroups are actually enforced; <code>pods<\/code> is the default.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#a-pvc-has-been-pending-for-ten-minutes-and-descr", "sec": "1.3", "q": "A PVC has been Pending for ten minutes and <code>describe<\/code> shows no events at all. Bug or not?", "a": "<p>Not a bug if the class uses <code>WaitForFirstConsumer<\/code> and nothing mounts the claim yet: no scheduling decision exists, so there is nothing to provision and nothing to report. It becomes a finding the moment a pod references it and the claim <em>stays<\/em> Pending; then the events appear and name the real reason.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#a-pv-sits-in-released-and-your-new-identical-pvc", "sec": "1.3", "q": "A PV sits in Released and your new, identical PVC will not bind to it. Why, and what is the fix?", "a": "<p>Retain leaves <code>spec.claimRef<\/code> pointing at the deleted claim, and a PV with a claimRef is spoken for. Clear it (<code>kubectl patch pv &lt;name&gt; -p '{\"spec\":{\"claimRef\":null}}'<\/code>) and it returns to Available. Deciding whether the old data should be wiped first is the actual judgment call.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#you-delete-a-statefulset-and-its-pvcs-remain-acc", "sec": "1.3", "q": "You delete a StatefulSet and its PVCs remain. Accident or design?", "a": "<p>Design. The claims outlive the set so scaling back up reattaches the same data, and so an accidental delete does not destroy the database. Newer clusters can opt in to cleanup via <code>persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy<\/code> (<code>whenDeleted<\/code> / <code>whenScaled<\/code>).<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#two-pods-on-different-nodes-must-share-a-directo", "sec": "1.3", "q": "Two pods on different nodes must share a directory. What do you need, and what will this lab do?", "a": "<p>A class whose provisioner supports RWX (NFS, CephFS, a cloud file service). local-path cannot, so the claim pends and the events say so. The honest lab answer is \"not possible here\" plus the fix you would apply in a real cluster, which is also the right exam answer when the storage cannot do what the task implies.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#which-quota-lines-cap-tenant-storage-and-why-is-", "sec": "1.3", "q": "Which quota lines cap tenant storage, and why is per-class quota interesting?", "a": "<p><code>requests.storage<\/code> and <code>persistentvolumeclaims<\/code> cap the total and the count; <code>&lt;class&gt;.storageclass.storage.k8s.io/requests.storage<\/code> caps a specific class. The per-class form is how a platform team offers a fast expensive tier without every tenant defaulting to it: a pricing decision expressed as a Kubernetes object.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#you-want-scale-down-to-reclaim-volumes-but-delet", "sec": "1.3", "q": "You want scale-down to reclaim volumes but deleting the StatefulSet by mistake to keep them. Which fields, and since when is this GA?", "a": "<p><code>persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy: { whenScaled: Delete, whenDeleted: Retain }<\/code>, GA since Kubernetes 1.32 (StatefulSetAutoDeletePVC). Delete works through an ownerReference on the PVC that the garbage collector honors after the pod is gone; a pod replaced after a node failure is not a scale event and keeps its claim.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#an-expansion-to-500gi-failed-because-the-backend", "sec": "1.3", "q": "An expansion to 500Gi failed because the backend has no capacity and the PVC keeps retrying. Can you back out?", "a": "<p>Yes: RecoverVolumeExpansionFailure is GA since 1.34, so edit the request down to any value still above <code>status.capacity<\/code> (never below it; shrinking is impossible). Watch <code>status.allocatedResourceStatuses<\/code> and events. Before 1.34 the recovery was the manual dance of Retain, delete the PVC, clear <code>claimRef<\/code>, recreate.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#restore-a-database-volume-from-last-night-s-snap", "sec": "1.3", "q": "Restore a database volume from last night's snapshot into a new PVC. What does the manifest look like and what must be true first?", "a": "<p>A PVC whose <code>dataSource<\/code> is <code>{ kind: VolumeSnapshot, name: &lt;snap&gt;, apiGroup: snapshot.storage.k8s.io }<\/code>, in the same namespace, with a StorageClass from the same CSI driver and a size at least the snapshot's restore size. The VolumeSnapshot must show <code>status.readyToUse: true<\/code>; a not-ready snapshot leaves the PVC Pending with a provisioner event saying so.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#how-do-you-change-a-volume-s-iops-tier-without-r", "sec": "1.3", "q": "How do you change a volume's IOPS tier without recreating it?", "a": "<p>VolumeAttributesClass (GA since 1.34): create a class with the driver's performance <code>parameters<\/code> and set <code>spec.volumeAttributesClassName<\/code> on the PVC; the field is mutable and the external-resizer applies the change. StorageClass parameters, by contrast, are fixed at provisioning time.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#a-tenant-applies-a-deployment-it-is-accepted-and", "sec": "1.4", "q": "A tenant applies a Deployment. It is accepted, and no pods appear. Where is the error?", "a": "<p>One level down, on the ReplicaSet: quota and PSS are pod-level admission decisions, and the Deployment controller only records the failure in the RS's events and conditions. <code>kubectl -n &lt;ns&gt; describe rs -l app=&lt;x&gt;<\/code> or namespace events. This indirection is a stock exam scenario.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#why-does-adding-a-limitrange-sometimes-make-prev", "sec": "1.4", "q": "Why does adding a LimitRange sometimes make previously-working pods fail?", "a": "<p>Because it enforces <code>min<\/code>/<code>max<\/code> as well as injecting defaults. Existing pods keep running (admission is not retroactive), but the next pod whose explicit requests fall outside the bounds is rejected, and the message comes from the LimitRange, not the quota.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#quota-says-requests-cpu-2-a-tenant-asks-why-they", "sec": "1.4", "q": "Quota says <code>requests.cpu: \"2\"<\/code>. A tenant asks why they cannot run 40 pods when the LimitRange injects 50m each.", "a": "<p>Because the other quota lines bind first: <code>limits.cpu: \"4\"<\/code> against a 200m default limit allows 20 pods, and <code>pods: \"20\"<\/code> caps it at the same number. Whichever binds first is the one named in the event. The lesson: quota is a conjunction, and the tightest line wins.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#name-the-isolation-ladder-and-one-cost-of-each-r", "sec": "1.4", "q": "Name the isolation ladder and one cost of each rung.", "a": "<p>Namespace + quota + policy (cheap, shared kernel and shared API server). Dedicated node pools via taints (no shared kernel; stranded capacity per pool). Virtual clusters (own API server and CRDs; more moving parts to run). Separate clusters (strongest; multiplied operational and idle cost). Pick the thinnest rung that satisfies the actual threat model.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#why-is-services-loadbalancers-1-in-a-tenant-quot", "sec": "1.4", "q": "Why is <code>services.loadbalancers: \"1\"<\/code> in a tenant quota?", "a": "<p>Because a LoadBalancer bills outside the cluster whether or not traffic flows. Quota here is a spend control, not a fairness control: the same reasoning that puts per-StorageClass storage quota in a tenant template.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#a-tenant-needs-to-install-its-own-crds-and-admis", "sec": "1.4", "q": "A tenant needs to install its own CRDs and admission webhooks but you cannot afford a cluster per tenant. Which model?", "a": "<p>A virtual control plane per tenant, for example vcluster: the tenant gets its own API server (so CRDs and webhooks are theirs alone) while pods still run on shared nodes through the syncer. Namespaces cannot isolate cluster-scoped objects; a dedicated cluster can, at full duplication cost.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#what-does-hnc-add-to-plain-namespaces-in-two-sen", "sec": "1.4", "q": "What does HNC add to plain namespaces, in two sentences?", "a": "<p>A parent-child hierarchy: RBAC, NetworkPolicies and other chosen objects propagate from a parent to all descendants, and users with rights in a parent can create subnamespaces without cluster-level permission. Tree labels on every namespace let selectors mean \"the whole team\", and HierarchicalResourceQuota caps a subtree.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#tenants-keep-launching-pods-with-the-prioritycla", "sec": "1.4", "q": "Tenants keep launching pods with the PriorityClass reserved for platform components. Fix with a quota.", "a": "<p>A ResourceQuota in each tenant namespace with <code>scopeSelector: { matchExpressions: [{ scopeName: PriorityClass, operator: In, values: [platform-critical] }] }<\/code> and <code>hard: { pods: \"0\" }<\/code>. Admission then rejects any pod naming that class in the namespace, with an \"exceeded quota\" message, while other classes are unaffected.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#why-should-a-storageclass-shared-between-tenants", "sec": "1.4", "q": "Why should a StorageClass shared between tenants use reclaimPolicy Delete?", "a": "<p>A Retain PV released by one tenant stays in the cluster with its data; PVs are cluster-scoped, so a claim from another namespace could bind to it once the claimRef is cleared. Delete removes the backend volume with the claim, so data cannot cross tenants through the storage layer. Per-tenant classes are the stronger alternative.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#a-namespace-runs-almost-no-traffic-and-still-top", "sec": "1.5", "q": "A namespace runs almost no traffic and still tops the cost report. Give the two most likely explanations.", "a": "<p>Oversized requests (billed on reservation, not usage), or expensive attached objects: LoadBalancers, provisioned volumes, retained snapshots. Check efficiency first: very low efficiency points at requests; high efficiency with high cost points at the objects.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#why-is-container-in-that-promql-query-not-option", "sec": "1.5", "q": "Why is <code>container!=\"\"<\/code> in that PromQL query not optional?", "a": "<p>cadvisor emits per-container series plus pod-level and node-level rollups with an empty <code>container<\/code> label, and often the pause container as <code>container=\"POD\"<\/code>. Summing everything counts the same CPU two or three times, so every aggregate over cadvisor series needs <code>{container!=\"\",container!=\"POD\"}<\/code> or an equivalent.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#should-idle-cost-be-spread-across-tenants-or-sho", "sec": "1.5", "q": "Should idle cost be spread across tenants or shown separately? Argue both.", "a": "<p>Spread: tenants see the true cost of the cluster they share, which discourages hoarding. Separate: the platform team owns bin-packing and node choice, so making idle visible as <em>their<\/em> number is what drives consolidation. Mature setups show both: tenant cost at request-level, plus a platform-owned idle line.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#you-cut-a-deployment-s-cpu-request-from-500m-to-", "sec": "1.5", "q": "You cut a deployment's CPU request from 500m to 50m and latency degrades. What happened?", "a": "<p>Requests set the cgroup CPU weight, so under contention the container now gets a much smaller share; and if a limit exists near the old request, throttling is likelier. Right-sizing means matching observed usage with headroom for peaks, not matching the average. Confirm with throttling metrics, not with the cost graph.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#give-the-four-step-right-sizing-loop-in-one-brea", "sec": "1.5", "q": "Give the four-step right-sizing loop in one breath.", "a": "<p>Measure usage → compare with requests → adjust requests → confirm no degradation (no throttling, no OOM, no Pending) and re-measure efficiency. The fourth step is the one that makes it engineering rather than cost-cutting.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#show-cost-per-team-when-teams-span-several-names", "sec": "1.5", "q": "Show cost per team when teams span several namespaces and pods carry a team label. Query?", "a": "<p><code>/allocation?window=7d&amp;aggregate=label:team<\/code> (or <code>kubectl cost label --opencost -l team<\/code>). Aggregation keys include <code>label:&lt;key&gt;<\/code> and <code>annotation:&lt;key&gt;<\/code>, so allocation follows whatever label discipline you enforce; a Kyverno policy requiring the label is what makes the report complete.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#what-does-shareidle-true-change-in-the-numbers-a", "sec": "1.5", "q": "What does shareIdle=true change in the numbers, and who should want it?", "a": "<p>The <code>__idle__<\/code> row disappears and its cost is distributed across the other rows in proportion to their own cost, per resource. Tenants who should feel the true cost of the cluster they share want it; a platform team that owns bin-packing wants <code>includeIdle=true<\/code> without sharing, so idle stays their line.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#where-do-on-prem-prices-come-from-and-what-is-th", "sec": "1.5", "q": "Where do on-prem prices come from, and what is the CPU default?", "a": "<p>Nodes with no cloud providerID use the <code>custom<\/code> provider and OpenCost's <code>default.json<\/code>: CPU 0.031611 per core-hour, RAM 0.004237 per GB-hour, GPU 0.95 per hour, described as based on GCP us-central1. Override them via the custom pricing ConfigMap or Helm values. The figures are notional; the ratios and efficiency numbers are what you read.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#a-workload-shows-totalefficiency-1-4-is-that-goo", "sec": "1.5", "q": "A workload shows totalEfficiency 1.4. Is that good?", "a": "<p>It means usage exceeded requests by 40%, so the workload is under-requested: it is being billed on usage, cheap on paper, but it is stealing headroom from neighbors and is first in line for eviction under memory pressure. Raise the request toward observed usage; efficiency near but below 1 is the target.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#state-the-four-opengitops-principles-without-loo", "sec": "2.1", "q": "State the four OpenGitOps principles without looking.", "a": "<p>Declarative; versioned and immutable; pulled automatically by agents; continuously reconciled. If you can only remember three, the one people drop is \"versioned and immutable\", which is also the one that carries the audit and rollback story.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#why-are-environment-branches-discouraged", "sec": "2.1", "q": "Why are environment branches discouraged?", "a": "<p>Long-lived branches accumulate divergent changes, so promotion becomes a merge with conflicts and cherry-picks rather than a reviewable diff, and the environments drift structurally rather than only in the values you meant to differ. Directories keep the difference explicit and diffable.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#a-colleague-fixes-production-with-kubectl-edit-d", "sec": "2.1", "q": "A colleague fixes production with <code>kubectl edit<\/code> during an incident. What happens next, and what should they have done?", "a": "<p>Self-heal reverts it at the next reconcile (or it is reported as drift). The sanctioned move is to suspend/disable reconciliation for that app, apply the emergency change, then land the same change in git and resume, so the fix survives and the history records it.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#what-does-prune-actually-change-and-what-is-the-", "sec": "2.1", "q": "What does prune actually change, and what is the failure mode in each setting?", "a": "<p>Prune deletes live resources whose manifests disappeared from git. Off: renamed or removed resources linger forever as invisible cruft. On: deleting a file deletes the running thing, so a careless refactor is a production outage. Neither is safe by default; the safety comes from knowing which one you have.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#where-do-secrets-live-in-a-gitops-repo", "sec": "2.1", "q": "Where do secrets live in a GitOps repo?", "a": "<p>Encrypted (Sealed Secrets or SOPS) or by reference (External Secrets pointing at a real store). Never plain, never base64-only. The distinction to state: Sealed Secrets/SOPS keep ciphertext in git; ESO keeps only a pointer and leaves truth in the external store.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#what-is-the-rendered-manifests-pattern-and-how-d", "sec": "2.1", "q": "What is the rendered manifests pattern and how does Argo CD 3.5 implement it?", "a": "<p>Render Helm or Kustomize in a pipeline and commit the resulting plain YAML to a branch or directory the agent syncs from, so reviewers diff final manifests. Argo CD's Source Hydrator (beta since 3.5) does it in the controller: <code>sourceHydrator.drySource<\/code> is the templated input, <code>syncSource.targetBranch<\/code>/<code>path<\/code> is where hydrated output is pushed and synced from, using a <code>repository-write<\/code> Secret.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#which-secret-approach-keeps-no-secret-material-i", "sec": "2.1", "q": "Which secret approach keeps no secret material in git at all, and what is its dependency?", "a": "<p>External Secrets Operator: git holds an ExternalSecret that names keys in a SecretStore or ClusterSecretStore; the operator fetches and writes the Kubernetes Secret on a refreshInterval. The dependency is the external store and its credential: if it is down, rotation stops, though already-created Secrets keep working. Sealed Secrets and SOPS keep ciphertext in git instead.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#what-does-flux-bootstrap-actually-create-and-wha", "sec": "2.1", "q": "What does flux bootstrap actually create, and what is the name of the object that then manages Flux itself?", "a": "<p>It installs the controllers, commits <code>gotk-components.yaml<\/code> and <code>gotk-sync.yaml<\/code> under <code>--path<\/code> in the repository, and creates a GitRepository and a Kustomization both named <code>flux-system<\/code> in the flux-system namespace. From then on that Kustomization reconciles the cluster directory, Flux included, so upgrades are a commit.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#why-does-helm-list-show-nothing-for-a-chart-argo", "sec": "2.1", "q": "Why does helm list show nothing for a chart Argo CD deployed?", "a": "<p>Argo CD renders the chart with <code>helm template<\/code> semantics and applies the manifests itself; it never writes Helm release Secrets. Flux's helm-controller, by contrast, performs real Helm install/upgrade actions and keeps release history, so <code>helm list -n &lt;storageNamespace&gt;<\/code> shows it. State that difference when comparing engines.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#an-app-is-synced-degraded-what-are-you-allowed-t", "sec": "2.2", "q": "An app is Synced/Degraded. What are you allowed to conclude, and what is the fix?", "a": "<p>Live matches git, so Argo CD has done its job; the desired state itself is broken (bad image, impossible resources, missing config key). The fix is a commit. Re-syncing changes nothing because the diff is already empty.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#a-sync-fails-with-field-is-immutable-two-ways-fo", "sec": "2.2", "q": "A sync fails with \"field is immutable\". Two ways forward?", "a": "<p>Set <code>Replace=true<\/code> (per app or per resource) so the sync uses <code>kubectl replace<\/code> instead of apply, or delete the resource and let Argo recreate it; <code>Force=true<\/code> combines the two. Choose deliberately: replace is not a free pass, since a Service manifest that omits <code>clusterIP<\/code> is still rejected as immutable, and a shrinking PVC is not going to be saved by either option.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#what-is-an-appproject-for-in-one-sentence-and-na", "sec": "2.2", "q": "What is an AppProject for, in one sentence, and name two things it constrains?", "a": "<p>It is the guardrail around a set of Applications: permitted source repos and permitted destinations (cluster + namespace), plus allowed/denied resource kinds, per-project RBAC roles and sync windows. It is the answer to \"let this team self-serve deployments without letting them deploy anything anywhere\".<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#you-are-handed-an-applicationset-with-a-git-dire", "sec": "2.2", "q": "You are handed an ApplicationSet with a git directory generator over <code>apps/*/overlays/*<\/code>. How many Applications will exist?", "a": "<p>One per matching directory in the repo at the current revision, so count the directories, then check the template for a name expression that could collide (two identical names silently overwrite). Predicting the exact set from the generator block is the testable skill; <code>kubectl -n argocd get applications<\/code> then confirms it.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#an-app-shows-outofsync-forever-on-a-field-no-hum", "sec": "2.2", "q": "An app shows OutOfSync forever on a field no human edits. What is happening and what fixes it?", "a": "<p>Something in-cluster mutates the resource after apply: a mutating webhook, an HPA writing replicas, a defaulting controller. Fix with <code>ignoreDifferences<\/code> for that path (or stop managing the field). This is a configuration decision, not a bug to chase.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#you-changed-the-repo-but-argo-insists-nothing-ch", "sec": "2.2", "q": "You changed the repo but Argo insists nothing changed. Sequence of moves?", "a": "<p><code>argocd app get --refresh<\/code> first (recompare), then <code>--hard-refresh<\/code> (drop the manifest cache), then check the app is on the branch you pushed to (<code>targetRevision<\/code>), then check repo-server logs for auth or render errors. Cheapest first, always.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#a-sync-ends-with-operation-phase-error-rather-th", "sec": "2.2", "q": "A sync ends with operation phase Error rather than Failed. What is the difference and where do you look?", "a": "<p><code>Failed<\/code> means the apply reached the API server and was rejected (immutable field, admission denial); the message is in <code>syncResult.resources[]<\/code>. <code>Error<\/code> means Argo CD itself could not complete the operation: rendering failed, the repo was unreachable, a hook could not be created. For Error, read repo-server and application-controller logs; for Failed, read the resource message.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#you-must-stop-managing-an-application-but-keep-i", "sec": "2.2", "q": "You must stop managing an Application but keep its workloads running. Exact move?", "a": "<p><code>argocd app delete X --cascade=false<\/code>, or with kubectl: patch <code>metadata.finalizers<\/code> to remove <code>resources-finalizer.argocd.argoproj.io<\/code>, then delete the Application. Deleting with the finalizer present cascades in the foreground and removes every managed resource. <code>Delete=false<\/code> as a resource annotation is the per-resource version of the same protection.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#an-applicationset-with-a-matrix-of-a-git-directo", "sec": "2.2", "q": "An ApplicationSet with a matrix of a git directories generator and a clusters generator: how many Applications, and what breaks if you nest another matrix inside?", "a": "<p>directories × clusters, one Application per pair, named by the template (collisions silently merge). Matrix takes exactly two child generators and combination generators nest only one level deep, so a matrix inside a matrix inside a matrix is rejected at generation time, not by the API server's schema validation.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#a-user-can-see-applications-and-sync-them-but-th", "sec": "2.2", "q": "A user can see Applications and sync them but the Logs tab is empty. Which policy line is missing since 3.0?", "a": "<p><code>p, role:&lt;their-role&gt;, logs, get, &lt;project&gt;/*, allow<\/code>. From 3.0, logs RBAC is enforced by default and is no longer implied by <code>applications, get<\/code>. The same 3.0 change split <code>update<\/code> and <code>delete<\/code> on child resources into <code>update/*<\/code> and <code>delete/*<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#flux-has-no-selfheal-setting-why-is-drift-still-", "sec": "2.3", "q": "Flux has no <code>selfHeal<\/code> setting. Why is drift still corrected?", "a": "<p>Because the Kustomization re-applies its rendered manifests every <code>interval<\/code>; correcting drift is a side effect of applying, not a separate feature. The corollary: your drift window is bounded by the interval, and shortening it costs API server load.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#a-kustomization-is-ready-false-name-the-three-la", "sec": "2.3", "q": "A Kustomization is Ready False. Name the three layers where it could have failed.", "a": "<p>Source (the GitRepository is not Ready: auth, ref, or network), build (kustomize build failed, bad path or invalid YAML), apply/health (the API server rejected something, or health checks timed out). The conditions and <code>flux logs<\/code> name the layer; do not skip straight to the manifests.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#how-do-you-order-install-crds-then-the-operator-", "sec": "2.3", "q": "How do you order \"install CRDs, then the operator, then the tenant CRs\" in Flux?", "a": "<p>Three Kustomizations with <code>dependsOn<\/code>, each with <code>wait: true<\/code> (or explicit <code>healthChecks<\/code>) so a dependency counts as satisfied only when its objects are actually healthy. Without wait/healthChecks, <code>dependsOn<\/code> only orders the <em>apply<\/em>, not the readiness.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#what-is-postbuild-substitute-for-and-what-is-its", "sec": "2.3", "q": "What is <code>postBuild.substitute<\/code> for, and what is its Argo CD analog?", "a": "<p>Injecting per-cluster or per-environment values into manifests after kustomize build, sourced inline or from ConfigMaps/Secrets. Argo's analogs are kustomize overlays, Helm values per Application, or ApplicationSet template parameters; Flux just lets you do it without another overlay directory.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#how-does-flux-stop-one-tenant-s-kustomization-fr", "sec": "2.3", "q": "How does Flux stop one tenant's Kustomization from deploying cluster-admin-level resources?", "a": "<p><code>spec.serviceAccountName<\/code>: the controller impersonates that ServiceAccount when applying, so the tenant's manifests can never exceed the tenant's RBAC. Combined with disallowing cross-namespace source references, that is Flux's multi-tenancy story: the structural equivalent of Argo's AppProject.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#a-helmrelease-shows-ready-false-with-reason-roll", "sec": "2.3", "q": "A HelmRelease shows Ready False with reason RollbackSucceeded. Is the release broken or healthy right now?", "a": "<p>Healthy on the last good revision: the upgrade failed and remediation rolled back. The object stays not-ready because the desired state (new chart or values) is not what is running. Fix the chart or values in git, or after fixing something out of band run <code>flux reconcile hr X --reset<\/code> to zero the retry counters if <code>RetriesExceeded<\/code> stopped further attempts.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#a-chart-upgrade-adds-a-crd-field-but-the-cr-that", "sec": "2.3", "q": "A chart upgrade adds a CRD field but the CR that uses it is rejected as unknown. Why, and which field fixes it?", "a": "<p><code>spec.upgrade.crds<\/code> defaults to <code>Skip<\/code>: Helm does not upgrade CRDs on upgrade, so the old schema is still served and prunes or rejects the new field. Set <code>upgrade.crds: CreateReplace<\/code> (and <code>install.crds: CreateReplace<\/code>), or deliver CRDs through their own Kustomization that the HelmRelease depends on.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#make-a-git-push-reconcile-flux-immediately-inste", "sec": "2.3", "q": "Make a git push reconcile Flux immediately instead of at the next interval. Objects and the field you hand to the git server?", "a": "<p>A <code>Receiver<\/code> of the matching <code>type<\/code> (github, gitea, gitlab, generic) with <code>events: [push]<\/code>, a <code>secretRef<\/code> holding a token, and <code>resources<\/code> listing the GitRepository. Expose the notification-controller's <code>webhook-receiver<\/code> Service, then configure the webhook URL as that host plus <code>status.webhookPath<\/code> with the same token as the secret.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#after-multi-tenant-lockdown-a-tenant-s-kustomiza", "sec": "2.3", "q": "After multi-tenant lockdown, a tenant's Kustomization fails with Forbidden. What did they leave out?", "a": "<p><code>spec.serviceAccountName<\/code>. With <code>--default-service-account=default<\/code>, a Kustomization or HelmRelease without an explicit account impersonates the namespace's <code>default<\/code> ServiceAccount, which has no RBAC; the API server refuses the apply and the Ready condition reads ReconciliationFailed with the Forbidden text. The platform must have created a ServiceAccount and RoleBinding for the tenant to name.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.4#two-tasks-in-a-pipeline-run-at-the-same-time-and", "sec": "2.4", "q": "Two tasks in a pipeline run at the same time and you did not expect it. Why?", "a": "<p>No <code>runAfter<\/code> and no result dependency between them, so Tekton runs them concurrently by design. Order comes from explicit <code>runAfter<\/code> or from consuming a result; nothing else implies sequence.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.4#how-do-files-get-from-the-clone-task-to-the-buil", "sec": "2.4", "q": "How do files get from the clone task to the build task, and what happens if you bind the workspace to an emptyDir?", "a": "<p>Through a workspace backed by a PVC or <code>volumeClaimTemplate<\/code>, mounted by both tasks. An emptyDir is per-pod, so each task gets an empty one and the build finds nothing: a classic \"why is my workspace empty\" bug.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.4#where-do-you-put-a-step-that-must-run-whether-th", "sec": "2.4", "q": "Where do you put a step that must run whether the pipeline passed or failed?", "a": "<p><code>spec.finally<\/code>. It runs after all other tasks regardless of outcome, and it can inspect <code>$(tasks.status)<\/code> to branch; cleanup, notifications, and teardown belong there rather than as a last <code>runAfter<\/code> task that never executes on failure.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.4#why-does-the-pipeline-push-to-kind-registry-5000", "sec": "2.4", "q": "Why does the pipeline push to <code>kind-registry:5000<\/code> and not <code>localhost:5001<\/code>?", "a": "<p>Because it runs in a pod, where <code>localhost<\/code> is the pod's own network namespace. The registry has an in-cluster DNS name wired into CoreDNS and containerd; the host reaches the same store through a published port. Same content, two names, and the digest is identical from both.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.4#your-eventlistener-is-ready-and-a-push-produces-", "sec": "2.4", "q": "Your EventListener is Ready and a push produces nothing. Diagnostic ladder?", "a": "<p>Did the webhook deliver (Gitea's webhook delivery log)? Did the listener receive it (its pod logs)? Did an interceptor filter it out (event type, secret mismatch, CEL expression)? Did the binding extract fields the template expects (a missing param yields an invalid PipelineRun)? Each rung produces a different error; check them in order.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.4#a-pipelinerun-shows-status-true-with-reason-comp", "sec": "2.4", "q": "A PipelineRun shows status True with reason Completed rather than Succeeded. What happened?", "a": "<p>It passed, but at least one task was skipped by a <code>when<\/code> expression; the skipped tasks and the expressions that evaluated false are listed under <code>status.skippedTasks<\/code>. Graders and scripts that check for <code>reason == Succeeded<\/code> will miss this, so check <code>status == \"True\"<\/code> on the Succeeded condition instead.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.4#which-spec-status-value-stops-new-tasks-lets-run", "sec": "2.4", "q": "Which spec.status value stops new tasks, lets running ones finish and still runs finally?", "a": "<p><code>StoppedRunFinally<\/code>. <code>CancelledRunFinally<\/code> kills running TaskRuns but still runs finally; plain <code>Cancelled<\/code> kills everything and skips finally. <code>PipelineRunPending<\/code> is the opposite direction: a run created but not yet started.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.4#a-step-referencing-a-stepaction-also-sets-image-", "sec": "2.4", "q": "A step referencing a StepAction also sets image. What happens, and why is the design that way?", "a": "<p>Validation error: a step with <code>ref<\/code> may not set image, command, args, script, env or volumeMounts, because the StepAction owns them and the step only supplies params and results wiring. That separation is what lets a platform team publish one vetted StepAction and let tenants compose Tasks around it without copying container details.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#you-set-setweight-10-with-3-replicas-and-no-traf", "sec": "2.5", "q": "You set <code>setWeight: 10<\/code> with 3 replicas and no traffic provider. What actually happens?", "a": "<p>Rollouts rounds to whole pods: it runs one canary pod alongside the stable set, so the real split is nearer 25–33%, not 10%. Exact weights require a traffic provider (Istio, Gateway API, NGINX) or more replicas.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#a-canary-aborted-and-the-analysisrun-shows-measu", "sec": "2.5", "q": "A canary aborted and the AnalysisRun shows measurements in phase Error. What do you check?", "a": "<p>The metric query and its provider: wrong series name, no data yet, unreachable Prometheus, or a selector that matches nothing. Errors count toward <code>consecutiveErrorLimit<\/code> (default 4), so a broken query aborts releases exactly like a bad release does; check the measurement message before touching the application.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#blue-green-with-autopromotionenabled-false-which", "sec": "2.5", "q": "Blue-green with <code>autoPromotionEnabled: false<\/code>: which Service points where, before and after promote?", "a": "<p>Before: active selects the old ReplicaSet's <code>rollouts-pod-template-hash<\/code>, preview selects the new one. After: both select the new hash, and the old ReplicaSet lingers for <code>scaleDownDelaySeconds<\/code> so rollback is a selector flip away.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#argo-cd-manages-your-rollout-you-abort-a-bad-can", "sec": "2.5", "q": "Argo CD manages your Rollout. You abort a bad canary. What happens next, and what should you do?", "a": "<p>The Rollout spec in git still names the bad image, so the next sync re-applies it and the rollout starts again. Revert the commit (or pin the tag back): abort is an operational stop, git is the desired state. Same lesson as any manual fix under GitOps.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#name-two-things-that-make-progressive-delivery-u", "sec": "2.5", "q": "Name two things that make progressive delivery unsafe regardless of tooling.", "a": "<p>Database or API changes that both versions cannot tolerate simultaneously (fix with expand-and-contract migrations and backwards-compatible contracts), and metrics too sparse or too coarse to detect harm within the canary window. A third: analysis that measures the stable version by accident.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#an-analysisrun-ended-inconclusive-and-the-rollou", "sec": "2.5", "q": "An AnalysisRun ended Inconclusive and the rollout stopped. Did it abort?", "a": "<p>No: inconclusive pauses the rollout at its current step and waits for a human to <code>promote<\/code> or <code>abort<\/code>. It happens when measurements satisfy neither <code>successCondition<\/code> nor <code>failureCondition<\/code> (or when a metric defines neither) more than <code>inconclusiveLimit<\/code> times. Failure aborts; error beyond <code>consecutiveErrorLimit<\/code> aborts; inconclusive asks.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#flagger-you-set-match-on-a-header-and-also-stepw", "sec": "2.5", "q": "Flagger: you set match on a header and also stepWeight/maxWeight. What runs?", "a": "<p>A/B testing: when Flagger finds an HTTP match condition it ignores <code>maxWeight<\/code> and <code>stepWeight<\/code> and runs <code>iterations<\/code> rounds with matched traffic routed to the canary. Remove <code>match<\/code> for a weighted canary; keep only <code>iterations<\/code> for blue/green.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#what-does-progressdeadlineseconds-do-on-a-plain-", "sec": "2.5", "q": "What does progressDeadlineSeconds do on a plain Deployment, and what does it not do?", "a": "<p>After 600 s (default) without progress it sets the Deployment condition <code>Progressing=False<\/code> with reason <code>ProgressDeadlineExceeded<\/code>, which is what Argo CD reads as Degraded. It does not roll back or pause anything; the old ReplicaSet keeps whatever pods <code>maxUnavailable<\/code> left it. Rollouts' <code>progressDeadlineAbort: true<\/code> is the version that also rolls back.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#why-must-canaryservice-and-stableservice-exist-w", "sec": "2.5", "q": "Why must canaryService and stableService exist when you add trafficRouting to a Rollout?", "a": "<p>The router splits traffic between two Services, so Rollouts needs one Service whose selector it points at the canary ReplicaSet and one for the stable set (it injects <code>rollouts-pod-template-hash<\/code> into their selectors). Without a router there is one Service and the split is by replica count, so neither field is needed.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.6#the-app-is-synced-and-healthy-but-the-feature-we", "sec": "2.6", "q": "\"The app is Synced and Healthy but the feature we merged an hour ago is missing.\"", "a": "<p>Bucket 4, stale. Check for suspension, then the last sync timestamp and the resolved revision against your commit SHA, then whether the app tracks the branch you pushed to. Refresh/reconcile before anything else.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.6#sync-fails-service-demo-is-invalid-spec-clusteri", "sec": "2.6", "q": "\"Sync fails: <code>Service \"demo\" is invalid: spec.clusterIP: Invalid value: field is immutable<\/code>.\"", "a": "<p>Bucket 2: the cluster refused the manifest. The text came from the API server, relayed by the sync operation. Fix the manifest, or force replace semantics if the field genuinely must change (accepting the churn that implies).<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.6#argocd-repo-server-logs-show-authentication-requ", "sec": "2.6", "q": "\"argocd-repo-server logs show <code>authentication required<\/code>; the workload is untouched and running fine.\"", "a": "<p>Bucket 3: access. No pod of yours is implicated; the controller's identity is. Repo credentials, token scope, or a rotated secret. The workload keeps running because reconciliation is what broke, not the deployment.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.6#deployment-exists-no-pods-no-error-on-the-deploy", "sec": "2.6", "q": "\"Deployment exists, no pods, no error on the Deployment.\"", "a": "<p>Look one level down at the ReplicaSet: quota, PSS, or an admission webhook rejected the pod template. Same indirection as sections 1.4 and 5.3. Not strictly a delivery bug: the delivery worked, admission refused.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.6#i-keep-deleting-this-configmap-and-it-keeps-comi", "sec": "2.6", "q": "\"I keep deleting this ConfigMap and it keeps coming back.\"", "a": "<p>Something reconciles it: self-heal, a Flux Kustomization at interval, or an operator that owns it. Prove which with <code>ownerReferences<\/code>, the <code>app.kubernetes.io/instance<\/code> label, or <code>flux trace<\/code>. Then change the source of truth instead of the live object, or suspend first if you need a temporary window.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.6#sync-failed-admission-webhook-validate-kyverno-s", "sec": "2.6", "q": "\"Sync failed: admission webhook \"validate.kyverno.svc-fail\" denied the request\". Which bucket, and where does the fix go?", "a": "<p>Bucket 5, admission. The manifest reached the API server and a policy engine refused it. Fix either the manifest so it satisfies the policy (the usual grading intent) or, if the task says the policy is wrong, the policy itself. Re-syncing changes nothing; Argo CD marks the resource SyncFailed, Flux reports ReconciliationFailed with the same text.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.6#a-flux-kustomization-reports-artifactfailed-what", "sec": "2.6", "q": "A Flux Kustomization reports ArtifactFailed. What is broken and where do you look first?", "a": "<p>The source it references has no artifact: the GitRepository or OCIRepository is not Ready (AuthenticationFailed, GitOperationFailed, bad ref) or has not fetched yet. Bucket 3, upstream of the Kustomization. <code>flux get sources all -A<\/code> and <code>kubectl describe gitrepository<\/code> first; the Kustomization will recover on its own once the source publishes.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.6#argo-cd-shows-sync-status-unknown-with-a-compari", "sec": "2.6", "q": "Argo CD shows sync status Unknown with a ComparisonError condition. Bucket?", "a": "<p>Bucket 3: comparison itself failed, so Argo does not even know whether live matches git. Causes are repo access (credentials, URL, revision), a render error in repo-server, or an unreachable destination cluster. The condition message names it; the workload is untouched, which is the tell.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#define-a-platform-in-the-paper-s-own-terms", "sec": "3.1", "q": "Define a platform in the paper's own terms.", "a": "<p>An integrated collection of capabilities, defined and presented according to the needs of its users. The emphasis on <em>users<\/em> and on <em>presentation<\/em> (interfaces) is what distinguishes a platform from a pile of infrastructure.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#what-is-the-thinnest-viable-platform-and-what-do", "sec": "3.1", "q": "What is the Thinnest Viable Platform, and what does it imply about build-vs-adopt?", "a": "<p>The smallest layer that provides consistency and accelerates delivery. It implies adopting existing capabilities wherever they exist and spending your effort on interfaces, integration and golden paths: the parts nobody can buy for your organization.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#a-team-can-request-a-namespace-through-a-form-an", "sec": "3.1", "q": "A team can request a namespace through a form, and someone approves it within a day. Is that self-service?", "a": "<p>No. The paper's definition requires fulfillment without a human in the loop. A form plus an approver is an interface over a ticket queue: operational maturity, not scalable. The fix is automated fulfillment with policy encoded in the API, and approval only where regulation demands it.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#name-the-dora-four-and-one-platform-specific-mea", "sec": "3.1", "q": "Name the DORA four and one platform-specific measure the paper adds.", "a": "<p>Deployment frequency, lead time for changes, time to restore service, change failure rate. The paper adds measures like latency from request to fulfillment of a capability, time to first contribution, and adoption, all platform-specific because they measure the platform, not the applications on it.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#which-platform-attribute-does-a-mandatory-non-co", "sec": "3.1", "q": "Which platform attribute does a mandatory, non-composable platform violate, and why does it matter practically?", "a": "<p>\"Optional and composable\". Practically: mandated platforms hide their own failures, because teams route around them quietly instead of filing the feedback that would improve them. Optionality is a feedback mechanism, not generosity.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#list-the-five-benefits-the-white-paper-says-ente", "sec": "3.1", "q": "List the five benefits the white paper says enterprises get from investing in platforms.", "a": "<p>Reduce cognitive load on product teams and so accelerate delivery; improve reliability and resiliency by dedicating experts to platform capabilities; accelerate delivery by reusing and sharing tools and knowledge across teams; reduce security, regulatory and functional risk by governing capabilities, users, tools and processes; enable cost-effective use of public cloud and managed services by delegating implementation while keeping control of the user experience.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#name-the-five-aspects-and-four-levels-of-the-pla", "sec": "3.1", "q": "Name the five aspects and four levels of the Platform Engineering Maturity Model.", "a": "<p>Aspects: Investment, Adoption, Interfaces, Operations, Measurement. Levels: Provisional, Operational, Scalable, Optimizing. Each aspect is scored on its own, and the model says the top level is not a goal in itself because each level demands more funding and time.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#which-glossary-role-decides-what-the-platform-sh", "sec": "3.1", "q": "Which glossary role decides what the platform should do, and which builds it?", "a": "<p>The platform product manager understands users, builds the roadmap and manages the team; the platform engineer develops and maintains interfaces and tools according to the product manager's requirements. Capability providers (internal teams or vendors) maintain the underlying capabilities; platform users are whoever runs software on the platform.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#an-organization-mandates-the-platform-for-produc", "sec": "3.1", "q": "An organization mandates the platform for production releases and runs office hours because users cannot find features. Which aspect and level?", "a": "<p>Adoption at level 2, Operational: extrinsic push (mandates and incentives), fragmented use, heavy reliance on help-desk style support. The adjacent target is level 3, intrinsic pull: documentation, ergonomic interfaces, shared roadmaps and feedback forums that make teams choose the platform on merit.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#a-user-says-their-field-does-not-work-kubectl-ge", "sec": "3.2", "q": "A user says their field \"does not work\". <code>kubectl get -o yaml<\/code> shows it missing entirely, and no error was printed. What happened?", "a": "<p>Pruning: the field is not in the schema, so the API server dropped it on write. Either add it to the schema or, rarely, mark that subtree with <code>x-kubernetes-preserve-unknown-fields<\/code>. The user experience argument is for adding it properly.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#your-controller-s-status-writes-vanish-one-likel", "sec": "3.2", "q": "Your controller's status writes vanish. One likely cause?", "a": "<p>The status subresource is enabled and the writer is going through the main endpoint, which ignores the <code>status<\/code> stanza. Use <code>UpdateStatus()<\/code> (or <code>kubectl patch --subresource=status<\/code>). The mirror-image bug also exists: with no subresource at all, a spec update from anyone overwrites the status your controller just wrote.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#you-must-forbid-changing-spec-tier-after-creatio", "sec": "3.2", "q": "You must forbid changing <code>spec.tier<\/code> after creation. How, without a webhook?", "a": "<p>A CEL validation on the field: <code>rule: \"self == oldSelf\"<\/code> with a message like \"tier is immutable\". <code>x-kubernetes-validations<\/code> is evaluated by the API server and can compare against the old object, which is exactly the immutability case.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#you-want-to-serve-v1alpha1-and-v1beta1-with-diff", "sec": "3.2", "q": "You want to serve <code>v1alpha1<\/code> and <code>v1beta1<\/code> with different fields. What do you need?", "a": "<p>Both versions <code>served<\/code>, exactly one marked <code>storage<\/code>, and a conversion strategy: <code>None<\/code> only if the fields are compatible, otherwise a conversion webhook. Everything read from etcd is stored in the storage version and converted on the way out.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#what-does-a-crd-give-you-for-free-and-what-does-", "sec": "3.2", "q": "What does a CRD give you for free, and what does it definitively not give you?", "a": "<p>Free: persistence, validation, defaulting, RBAC integration, watch/informers, <code>kubectl<\/code> support and explain docs, quota counting, policy-engine visibility. Not free: any behavior whatsoever. Nothing happens until a controller acts on the object.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#your-cel-rule-over-a-list-of-strings-is-rejected", "sec": "3.2", "q": "Your CEL rule over a list of strings is rejected with \"exceeded budget by more than 100x\". What do you change?", "a": "<p>Bound the inputs the rule iterates: <code>maxItems<\/code> on the array and <code>maxLength<\/code> on the string items (or <code>maxProperties<\/code> on a map). The estimator assumes worst-case sizes for unbounded collections, so the rule itself can stay as it is. The limits also become real validation, which is usually what a platform API wants anyway.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#you-tightened-a-pattern-on-spec-name-existing-ob", "sec": "3.2", "q": "You tightened a pattern on spec.name. Existing objects with old-style names must still be updatable. What makes that work, and what does it not cover?", "a": "<p>Validation ratcheting, GA since 1.33: an update is accepted if every part that fails validation is unchanged by that update. It does not ratchet transition rules (<code>oldSelf<\/code>), list-type changes, changes to <code>required<\/code>, renamed properties or metadata rules, and it never applies to creates. For finer control write the grandfathering into the rule with <code>optionalOldSelf: true<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#you-want-to-drop-v1alpha1-from-a-crd-but-the-upd", "sec": "3.2", "q": "You want to drop v1alpha1 from a CRD but the update is refused. Why, and what is the sequence?", "a": "<p><code>status.storedVersions<\/code> still lists v1alpha1, meaning objects may be stored at it; the API server refuses to remove a version that stored data might need. Make v1 the storage version, rewrite every object (a StorageVersionMigration or a no-op update loop) so they are stored at v1, patch <code>status.storedVersions<\/code> to <code>[v1]<\/code>, then remove v1alpha1 from <code>spec.versions<\/code>. Setting <code>served: false<\/code> first is the polite intermediate step.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#enable-kubectl-get-widgets-field-selector-spec-t", "sec": "3.2", "q": "Enable kubectl get widgets --field-selector spec.tier=gold. Field, version status, and limits?", "a": "<p><code>spec.versions[].selectableFields: [{jsonPath: .spec.tier}]<\/code>, GA since Kubernetes 1.32. Only scalar fields, at most 8 per version, and the selector must be written against the version that declares them. It replaces the habit of copying spec fields into labels purely so controllers and humans can filter.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#you-patch-a-cr-and-its-status-still-reports-the-", "sec": "3.3", "q": "You patch a CR and its status still reports the old problem. What do you check before believing it?", "a": "<p><code>status.observedGeneration<\/code> against <code>metadata.generation<\/code>. If they differ, the controller has not processed your edit yet and the status describes the previous spec. If they match and the status is still wrong, now it is a real finding.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#a-namespace-has-been-terminating-for-twenty-minu", "sec": "3.3", "q": "A namespace has been Terminating for twenty minutes. Method?", "a": "<p>Find what is left (<code>kubectl api-resources --verbs=list --namespaced -o name | xargs -n1 kubectl get -n &lt;ns&gt;<\/code>), find the finalizer on it, and find whether the owning controller is running. The fix is usually reviving the controller; stripping the finalizer is a last resort that leaks whatever it was cleaning up.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#why-is-deleting-an-operator-managed-pod-not-a-wa", "sec": "3.3", "q": "Why is deleting an operator-managed pod not a way to \"reset\" anything?", "a": "<p>Because reconciliation is level-based: the controller observes that a pod is missing and creates one, with no memory of the event. If you need a different outcome, change the spec (or the underlying cause); the loop will faithfully restore whatever the spec says forever.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#an-operator-s-crs-apply-fine-and-nothing-happens", "sec": "3.3", "q": "An operator's CRs apply fine and nothing happens at all. Two checks?", "a": "<p>Is the controller running (and did it win leader election)? And does its RBAC cover your namespace and your kind? A Forbidden in the controller's logs is the classic silent failure. Only after both: is the CR written in a way the controller ignores (a selector, a class field, a paused annotation)?<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#name-the-five-ways-an-operator-integrates-with-t", "sec": "3.3", "q": "Name the five ways an operator integrates with the rest of a platform.", "a": "<p>Its CRs are delivered by GitOps; its metrics are scraped and alerted on; its connection Secrets are consumed by apps (or synced by ESO); its CRs are subject to policy at admission; and its API is fronted by a thinner developer-facing abstraction (Crossplane/kro/portal template). That list is the \"integration\" half of the competency.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#an-operator-deployment-has-three-replicas-and-on", "sec": "3.3", "q": "An operator Deployment has three replicas and only one does work. How does it decide, and how do you see which?", "a": "<p>Leader election on a Lease object in the operator's namespace (LeaderElectionID as the name; 15 s lease, 10 s renew deadline by default). <code>kubectl -n &lt;ns&gt; get lease &lt;id&gt; -o jsonpath='{.spec.holderIdentity}'<\/code> names the leading pod. Non-leaders run their caches but skip reconciling, so logs on the wrong pod show nothing.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#what-does-owns-in-a-controller-builder-do-and-wh", "sec": "3.3", "q": "What does Owns() in a controller builder do, and what does it explain about operator behavior you see in the cluster?", "a": "<p>It watches the child kind and, when a child changes, enqueues the owner found through its controller ownerReference. That is why deleting an operator-created Deployment or Secret triggers an immediate recreation, why ownerReferences are the map of what an operator manages, and why the operator needs full RBAC on every owned kind.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#install-an-operator-with-olm-v1-pinned-to-versio", "sec": "3.3", "q": "Install an operator with OLM v1 pinned to versions below 2.0 from channel stable. Which object, and what does upgradeConstraintPolicy: SelfCertified change?", "a": "<p>A <code>ClusterExtension<\/code> with <code>spec.namespace<\/code>, <code>spec.serviceAccount.name<\/code> (an SA you created with the bundle's RBAC), and <code>spec.source.catalog: { packageName, channels: [stable], version: \"&lt;2.0\" }<\/code>. The default <code>CatalogProvided<\/code> allows only upgrades the catalog's graph declares; <code>SelfCertified<\/code> allows any version including downgrades, and the CRD upgrade safety preflight still runs unless disabled.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#why-should-a-condition-be-named-ready-or-degrade", "sec": "3.3", "q": "Why should a condition be named Ready or Degraded rather than Deploying, and what is polarity?", "a": "<p>Condition types describe an observed state (adjective or past participle), and a transition is expressed by toggling status, so <code>Reconciling=True<\/code> rather than a <code>Deploying<\/code> type. Polarity is whether True is the normal state (<code>Ready<\/code>) or the abnormal one (<code>Degraded<\/code>, <code>Stalled<\/code>); a consumer must know it per type, which is why a summary <code>Ready<\/code> condition exists.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#which-template-type-creates-kubernetes-objects-a", "sec": "3.4", "q": "Which template type creates Kubernetes objects, and what is its idempotency story?", "a": "<p><code>resource<\/code>. With <code>action: create<\/code> a re-run fails on AlreadyExists; with <code>action: apply<\/code> it converges. Workflows do not reconcile, so if the same request may be submitted twice, you design for it: apply semantics, or a guard step.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#a-workflow-node-says-forbidden-where-do-you-look", "sec": "3.4", "q": "A workflow node says <code>forbidden<\/code>. Where do you look, and what is the classic first offender?", "a": "<p>The workflow's ServiceAccount and its bindings, not the workflow spec. The classic first offender in Argo is missing <code>create,patch<\/code> on <code>workflowtaskresults.argoproj.io<\/code>, the executor's own reporting channel, which fails before your intended resource permission is even exercised.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#when-is-a-workflow-the-wrong-tool-and-what-repla", "sec": "3.4", "q": "When is a workflow the wrong tool, and what replaces it?", "a": "<p>When the desired state must stay true indefinitely; a workflow runs once and forgets. Use a controller/operator, or a declarative composition engine (Crossplane, kro) whose CR keeps reconciling. Workflows are for sequences with an end.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#two-dag-tasks-with-no-dependencies-what-happens-", "sec": "3.4", "q": "Two DAG tasks with no <code>dependencies<\/code>: what happens, and how do you gate one on a condition?", "a": "<p>They run in parallel. Gate with <code>dependencies<\/code> for ordering and <code>when<\/code> for conditions (evaluated against parameters or a previous task's result). A validation node that everything depends on is the standard \"fail before side effects\" pattern.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#what-turns-a-workflow-into-a-self-service-produc", "sec": "3.4", "q": "What turns a Workflow into a self-service product?", "a": "<p>Promotion to a WorkflowTemplate with typed, documented, defaulted parameters, a submit surface (UI, CLI, portal, or a Backstage action), input validation before side effects, and an auditable record of who requested what. Same content, packaged as an interface.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#a-step-must-run-after-build-whether-build-succee", "sec": "3.4", "q": "A step must run after <code>build<\/code> whether <code>build<\/code> succeeded or failed, but only if <code>lint<\/code> succeeded. How do you express that in a DAG?", "a": "<p>Use <code>depends<\/code> rather than <code>dependencies<\/code>: <code>depends: \"(build.Succeeded || build.Failed) &amp;&amp; lint.Succeeded\"<\/code>. The result operands (<code>.Succeeded<\/code>, <code>.Failed<\/code>, <code>.Errored<\/code>, <code>.Skipped<\/code>, <code>.Omitted<\/code>, <code>.Daemoned<\/code>) are what make outcome-based ordering possible; <code>dependencies<\/code> can only say \"after it finished successfully\".<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#two-provisioning-runs-for-the-same-tenant-must-n", "sec": "3.4", "q": "Two provisioning runs for the same tenant must never execute concurrently, and a nightly job must skip its tick if the previous one is still running. Fields?", "a": "<p>A workflow-level <code>synchronization.mutexes<\/code> entry named per tenant (or a ConfigMap-backed <code>semaphores<\/code> entry of size 1) serializes submissions in a namespace. For the schedule, <code>CronWorkflow.spec.concurrencyPolicy: Forbid<\/code>; <code>Replace<\/code> would kill the running one instead. A waiting Workflow shows <code>Pending<\/code> with a message naming the lock, not a scheduling error.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#you-submit-a-workflow-with-serviceaccountname-se", "sec": "3.4", "q": "You submit a Workflow with <code>serviceAccountName<\/code> set and the controller rejects it, although the SA exists. Likely cause?", "a": "<p>The controller runs with <code>workflowRestrictions.templateReferencing: Strict<\/code> or <code>Secure<\/code>: submissions must use <code>workflowTemplateRef<\/code> and may only override an allow-listed set of fields (<code>arguments<\/code>, <code>entrypoint<\/code>, GC settings and a few more). Security-sensitive fields such as <code>serviceAccountName<\/code>, <code>volumes<\/code> or <code>podSpecPatch<\/code> must live in the WorkflowTemplate. Move the field there and resubmit.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#what-does-an-exit-handler-see-that-a-normal-temp", "sec": "3.4", "q": "What does an exit handler see that a normal template does not, and what is the difference between <code>argo stop<\/code> and <code>argo terminate<\/code>?", "a": "<p>An <code>onExit<\/code> template (or <code>hooks.exit<\/code>) runs after the entrypoint and can read <code>{{workflow.status}}<\/code> (<code>Succeeded<\/code>, <code>Failed<\/code>, <code>Error<\/code>) and <code>{{workflow.failures}}<\/code>, so it can branch with <code>when<\/code>. <code>argo stop<\/code> stops the running nodes and still runs exit handlers; <code>argo terminate<\/code> kills everything and skips them, which is why cleanup logic belongs in a hook you know will run.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#name-the-three-objects-and-who-owns-each", "sec": "3.5", "q": "Name the three objects and who owns each.", "a": "<p>XRD (platform team: the promised API and its schema), Composition (platform team: the recipe, changeable without touching the API), XR (developer: an instance). The generated CRD is Crossplane's doing, not yours, which is the whole labor saving.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#an-xr-is-stuck-not-ready-and-the-message-is-unhe", "sec": "3.5", "q": "An XR is stuck not-Ready and the message is unhelpful. Diagnostic order?", "a": "<p>Packages first (<code>providers<\/code>, <code>functions<\/code> Installed + Healthy), then <code>crossplane resource trace<\/code>, then the composed resources' own conditions, then the underlying API error inside them. Bottom-up, because that is the direction failure propagates.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#why-can-a-namespaced-xr-not-compose-a-cluster-sc", "sec": "3.5", "q": "Why can a namespaced XR not compose a cluster-scoped resource directly?", "a": "<p>Ownership rules: a namespaced object cannot own a cluster-scoped one, and Crossplane relies on ownerReferences for lifecycle. The workaround the lab uses is a namespaced <code>Object<\/code> (provider-kubernetes) that <em>contains<\/em> the cluster-scoped manifest, authenticated with a <code>ClusterProviderConfig<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#you-edit-a-composition-what-happens-to-the-twelv", "sec": "3.5", "q": "You edit a Composition. What happens to the twelve XRs already using it?", "a": "<p>They all reconcile to the new recipe, without anyone touching them: powerful and risky in equal measure. If that matters, pin instances to a composition revision or version the composition and migrate deliberately.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#when-would-you-choose-kro-or-a-plain-operator-ov", "sec": "3.5", "q": "When would you choose kro or a plain operator over Crossplane?", "a": "<p>kro when you want the same declarative expansion with far less machinery and can accept a young API (3.6). A plain operator when the value is domain behavior over time (failover, backup, upgrade orchestration) rather than composing existing resources. Crossplane's sweet spot is composing many things, including external cloud APIs, behind one abstraction.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#an-xr-shows-synced-true-ready-false-another-show", "sec": "3.5", "q": "An XR shows <code>Synced=True<\/code>, <code>Ready=False<\/code>. Another shows <code>Synced=False<\/code>. What is each telling you?", "a": "<p><code>Synced=False<\/code>: Crossplane could not render or apply the composition (unknown function, bad patch path, forbidden, invalid manifest); the message on the XR names it and the composed resources may not exist yet. <code>Synced=True, Ready=False<\/code>: the recipe applied, but a composed resource is not ready; descend into that resource's own conditions (<code>crossplane resource trace<\/code> draws the tree). The first is a platform-team bug, the second is usually the provider or the external system.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#you-must-import-an-existing-cloud-database-into-", "sec": "3.5", "q": "You must import an existing cloud database into Crossplane without risking any change to it. Fields?", "a": "<p>A managed resource with <code>spec.managementPolicies: [\"Observe\"]<\/code> and the <code>crossplane.io/external-name<\/code> annotation set to the real resource's identifier. Crossplane populates <code>status.atProvider<\/code> and never creates, updates or deletes. Later you can widen the policy (add <code>Update<\/code>, <code>Delete<\/code>) to take over management step by step.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#which-one-field-on-the-xrd-makes-every-instance-", "sec": "3.5", "q": "Which one field on the XRD makes every instance use one specific Composition regardless of what the XR asks for, and what happens to <code>compositionRef<\/code> on the XR?", "a": "<p><code>spec.enforcedCompositionRef.name<\/code>. It overrides <code>compositionRef<\/code>, <code>compositionSelector<\/code> and <code>defaultCompositionRef<\/code>; Crossplane sets the XR's <code>compositionRef<\/code> to the enforced Composition. <code>defaultCompositionRef<\/code> only fills a blank at creation time and never overrides an explicit choice.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#a-provider-shows-healthy-false-name-the-three-us", "sec": "3.5", "q": "A provider shows <code>HEALTHY: False<\/code>. Name the three usual causes and where each is visible.", "a": "<p>Image pull (a private registry without <code>packagePullSecrets<\/code>): visible on the revision's conditions and the provider pod events. Unsatisfied dependency or Crossplane version constraint: the <code>ProviderRevision<\/code> condition reason <code>UnhealthyPackageRevision<\/code> and the <code>Lock<\/code> object. The provider pod crashing: plain pod diagnosis in <code>crossplane-system<\/code>. If the package is healthy but MRs are <code>Synced=False<\/code> with <code>forbidden<\/code>, the problem is RBAC, not the package.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#how-does-kro-know-to-create-the-namespace-before", "sec": "3.6", "q": "How does kro know to create the namespace before the quota?", "a": "<p>From the reference: the quota template uses <code>${ns.metadata.name}<\/code>, so kro builds a dependency graph from the CEL references and orders accordingly. You never write <code>dependsOn<\/code>, which also means a refactor that removes a reference can silently reorder your graph.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#a-team-asks-for-a-portal-so-developers-can-self-", "sec": "3.6", "q": "A team asks for \"a portal so developers can self-serve\". What do you ask before agreeing?", "a": "<p>What happens after the form is submitted. If fulfillment is still manual, the portal is a prettier ticket queue; the automation (templates → git → GitOps → controllers) is the actual work, and the portal is the last 10%. That is the white paper's interface-versus-capability distinction.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#nightly-image-rebuild-and-rescan-which-engine-an", "sec": "3.6", "q": "Nightly image rebuild-and-rescan: which engine, and why not the others?", "a": "<p>A CronWorkflow: scheduled, run-to-completion, DAG with retries and notifications. Not an operator (nothing to reconcile forever), not Tekton (no git event trigger, though it could run the pipeline), not Crossplane (no resource graph to maintain).<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#give-the-three-questions-that-decide-the-engine", "sec": "3.6", "q": "Give the three questions that decide the engine.", "a": "<p>Does state need reconciling forever or producing once? What is the trigger: API object, schedule, git event, human? Who is the audience: YAML author, form filler, or another system? Answer those three and the table picks itself.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#what-does-the-scm-provider-generator-actually-wa", "sec": "3.6", "q": "What does the SCM-provider generator actually watch, and how would you debug a repo that never appears?", "a": "<p>It queries the git host's API for repos in an org matching filters (name pattern, topic, path existence), then templates one Application per match. Debug by reading the generator block (org, filters, credentials Secret), then checking the ApplicationSet's status/conditions and controller logs for API errors, usually a token scope or a filter that does not match.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#an-rgd-is-active-but-every-instance-sits-with-re", "sec": "3.6", "q": "An RGD is <code>Active<\/code>, but every instance sits with <code>ResourcesReady=False<\/code> and the kro controller logs <code>forbidden<\/code>. Cause?", "a": "<p>kro runs in RBAC <code>aggregation<\/code> mode and nobody granted the controller rights on the kind the RGD templates. The RGD passes static analysis (so it is Active) but the controller cannot create the child objects. Add a ClusterRole for that kind labeled <code>rbac.kro.run/aggregate-to-controller: \"true\"<\/code>. Same failure family as a Crossplane provider without RBAC or a workflow SA without <code>workflowtaskresults<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#a-backstage-template-s-publish-step-fails-with-a", "sec": "3.6", "q": "A Backstage template's publish step fails with an authentication error although the form rendered fine. Where do you look, and what is the other common form-time failure?", "a": "<p>The scaffolder backend's integration configuration (<code>integrations.github|gitlab|gitea<\/code> in <code>app-config.yaml<\/code>) and the token it holds: repo-creation rights on the target org. The form-time failure is <code>RepoUrlPicker<\/code> with <code>allowedHosts<\/code> not listing your git host, which blocks submission before any step runs.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#how-do-you-make-a-kro-resource-wait-for-a-deploy", "sec": "3.6", "q": "How do you make a kro resource wait for a Deployment to actually be available before creating the Service that points at it, and why is the default insufficient?", "a": "<p>Add <code>readyWhen: [${deployment.status.availableReplicas &gt; 0}]<\/code> (or a check on the <code>Available<\/code> condition) to the Deployment resource. By default a resource is \"ready\" as soon as it exists and its expressions resolve, so dependents proceed even if the pods never start. <code>includeWhen<\/code> is the different question of whether to create the resource at all.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#which-backstage-kinds-must-a-catalog-contain-bef", "sec": "3.6", "q": "Which Backstage kinds must a catalog contain before <code>spec.owner: group:default/team-x<\/code> resolves, and what happens if they are missing?", "a": "<p>A <code>Group<\/code> named <code>team-x<\/code> (and usually <code>User<\/code> entities in <code>memberOf<\/code>). Without it the Component still ingests, but the <code>ownedBy<\/code> relation dangles: the UI shows a broken owner link and ownership filters miss the entity. Groups and Users usually come from an org provider (GitHub, LDAP) rather than hand-written YAML.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.1#your-servicemonitor-is-correct-and-no-target-app", "sec": "4.1", "q": "Your ServiceMonitor is correct and no target appears. List the checks in order.", "a": "<p>1) Prometheus's <code>serviceMonitorSelector<\/code> and <code>serviceMonitorNamespaceSelector<\/code>: does it even consider your monitor? 2) Does the monitor's selector match the Service's labels? 3) Is <code>endpoints[].port<\/code> the port <em>name<\/em>, and does the Service name it? 4) Does the Service have ready endpoints? 5) RBAC and NetworkPolicy between monitoring and the target namespace.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.1#why-is-sum-rate-x-5m-right-and-rate-sum-x-5m-wro", "sec": "4.1", "q": "Why is <code>sum(rate(x[5m]))<\/code> right and <code>rate(sum(x)[5m])<\/code> wrong?", "a": "<p>Counters reset per series; <code>rate<\/code> knows how to handle resets within a single series. Summing first merges series (and their resets) into a meaningless line, and the syntax is invalid anyway without a subquery. Rate first, aggregate second, always.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.1#how-do-you-alert-on-the-metric-disappeared-entir", "sec": "4.1", "q": "How do you alert on \"the metric disappeared entirely\"?", "a": "<p><code>absent(up{job=\"x\"} == 1)<\/code> or <code>absent(metric)<\/code>: an ordinary comparison returns no series when there is nothing to compare, so it can never fire. <code>absent()<\/code> exists precisely to turn \"no data\" into a value of 1.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.1#a-p95-query-returns-nan-two-plausible-reasons", "sec": "4.1", "q": "A p95 query returns NaN. Two plausible reasons?", "a": "<p>No observations in the window (nothing has been recorded yet, so all buckets are empty), or you aggregated away the <code>le<\/code> label that <code>histogram_quantile<\/code> needs. The form that keeps it: <code>histogram_quantile(0.95, sum by (le) (rate(x_bucket[5m])))<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.1#an-exporter-added-a-label-with-one-value-per-req", "sec": "4.1", "q": "An exporter added a label with one value per request. What happens, and what is the fix?", "a": "<p>Cardinality explosion: a new series per unique value, memory and TSDB growth, slow queries. Fix at ingestion with a <code>metricRelabeling<\/code> that drops the label or the metric, and upstream by removing it from the exporter. High-cardinality labels (user IDs, URLs with IDs, trace IDs) belong in logs and traces, not metric labels.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.1#you-need-to-scrape-a-database-vm-outside-the-clu", "sec": "4.1", "q": "You need to scrape a database VM outside the cluster with the Prometheus Operator, without editing the Prometheus config by hand. Which CRD, and which selector must match?", "a": "<p>A <code>ScrapeConfig<\/code> with <code>staticConfigs[].targets: [\"db.example.internal:9187\"]<\/code> (or an <code>httpSDConfigs<\/code>/<code>dnsSDConfigs<\/code> entry), labeled to match the Prometheus object's <code>scrapeConfigSelector<\/code>, in a namespace its <code>scrapeConfigNamespaceSelector<\/code> covers. If that selector is nil, the ScrapeConfig must live in Prometheus's own namespace.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.1#predict-that-a-volume-fills-within-a-day-and-ale", "sec": "4.1", "q": "Predict that a volume fills within a day, and alert if any series that should exist has vanished. Two expressions?", "a": "<p><code>predict_linear(node_filesystem_avail_bytes{mountpoint=\"/data\"}[6h], 24*3600) &lt; 0<\/code> for the volume (a gauge extrapolated linearly). <code>absent_over_time(up{job=\"payments\"}[10m])<\/code> for the disappearance: it yields 1 only when no sample existed in the window, whereas a plain comparison returns nothing when there is nothing to compare.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.1#a-servicemonitor-in-monitoring-selects-the-right", "sec": "4.1", "q": "A ServiceMonitor in <code>monitoring<\/code> selects the right labels and port for Services in <code>argocd<\/code>, yet no target appears and Prometheus's own selectors are <code>{}<\/code>. What is missing?", "a": "<p><code>spec.namespaceSelector<\/code> on the ServiceMonitor itself: <code>matchNames: [argocd]<\/code> or <code>any: true<\/code>. Omitted, it means \"Services in my own namespace\", so the monitor matches nothing in <code>argocd<\/code>. Prometheus's <code>serviceMonitorNamespaceSelector<\/code> decides which monitors are read; the monitor's own <code>namespaceSelector<\/code> decides which Services it may reach.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#an-alert-shows-firing-in-prometheus-and-nothing-", "sec": "4.2", "q": "An alert shows Firing in Prometheus and nothing arrived. Three candidate causes?", "a": "<p>A silence matches it; an inhibition rule suppresses it; or routing sent it to a receiver that is failing (check Alertmanager's own logs and its <code>alertmanager_notifications_failed_total<\/code>). A fourth, if it only just fired: <code>group_wait<\/code> has not elapsed.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#your-rule-applies-cleanly-and-never-evaluates-wh", "sec": "4.2", "q": "Your rule applies cleanly and never evaluates. What did you not check?", "a": "<p>The Prometheus object's <code>ruleSelector<\/code> (and its namespace selector). Unmatched rules are ignored silently, the same way ServiceMonitors fail, with the same first command: read the selector.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#silence-versus-inhibition-when-do-you-use-each", "sec": "4.2", "q": "Silence versus inhibition: when do you use each?", "a": "<p>Silence: temporary, human-initiated, targeted at a known maintenance or a known-noisy alert, with an expiry and a comment. Inhibition: a standing rule expressing a relationship: when the cause alert fires, suppress its downstream symptoms. Silences are operations; inhibitions are design.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#how-long-roughly-from-condition-becomes-true-to-", "sec": "4.2", "q": "How long, roughly, from \"condition becomes true\" to \"notification sent\" on a stock stack?", "a": "<p>Up to one evaluation interval (30s) to notice, plus <code>for<\/code> (say 1–5m), plus <code>group_wait<\/code> (30s). So a couple of minutes minimum, which is why you wait before declaring an alerting pipeline broken, and why <code>for: 15m<\/code> on a page-worthy symptom is usually too slow.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#what-is-the-watchdog-alert-for", "sec": "4.2", "q": "What is the Watchdog alert for?", "a": "<p>It fires permanently as a dead-man's switch: an external system expects to keep receiving it and alerts when it stops, catching the failure mode where Prometheus or Alertmanager itself dies and therefore cannot alert you about anything. Self-monitoring has to come from outside.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#notify-the-database-team-only-during-business-ho", "sec": "4.2", "q": "\"Notify the database team only during business hours, page everyone else at any time.\" Which fields carry that?", "a": "<p>A top-level <code>time_intervals<\/code> entry (<code>times<\/code> 09:00-17:00, <code>weekdays<\/code> monday:friday, a <code>location<\/code>), and on the database route <code>active_time_intervals: [business_hours]<\/code>, which suppresses notifications outside the window. The other routes carry no interval. <code>mute_time_intervals<\/code> is the inverse (suppress while it matches); if both are present on one route, mute wins.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#a-team-s-alertmanagerconfig-in-namespace-team-a-", "sec": "4.2", "q": "A team's <code>AlertmanagerConfig<\/code> in namespace <code>team-a<\/code> routes <code>alertname=\"NodeDown\"<\/code> to their webhook, is selected, appears in the rendered config, and never fires. Why?", "a": "<p>The operator scopes merged AlertmanagerConfigs with an added <code>namespace=\"team-a\"<\/code> matcher on every route it contributes; <code>NodeDown<\/code> carries no such label, so the route can never match. Cluster-wide alerts must be routed from the global config (<code>spec.configSecret<\/code> or <code>spec.alertmanagerConfiguration<\/code>), where the namespace matcher is not enforced.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#explain-the-14-4-in-a-burn-rate-alert-and-why-th", "sec": "4.2", "q": "Explain the 14.4 in a burn-rate alert and why the rule has two windows.", "a": "<p>14.4 is the burn rate at which 2% of a 30-day budget is consumed in one hour (30 days × 24 hours × 0.02 = 14.4). The long window (1h) decides that the burn is sustained; the short window (5m) must also exceed it so the alert stops as soon as the error rate falls, instead of firing for the rest of the hour on old data. Both conditions are joined with <code>and<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#how-do-you-prove-a-new-alerting-rule-fires-under", "sec": "4.2", "q": "How do you prove a new alerting rule fires under the right conditions without waiting for production to break?", "a": "<p><code>promtool test rules<\/code> with a test file: <code>rule_files<\/code>, <code>evaluation_interval<\/code>, <code>input_series<\/code> with synthetic <code>values<\/code>, and <code>alert_rule_test<\/code> cases asserting <code>exp_alerts<\/code> (labels and annotations) at a given <code>eval_time<\/code>. Pair it with <code>promtool check rules<\/code> for syntax, and <code>amtool config routes test<\/code> to prove where the labels route.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.3#how-does-a-dashboard-get-into-grafana-in-a-gitop", "sec": "4.3", "q": "How does a dashboard get into Grafana in a GitOps platform, and why not click it?", "a": "<p>JSON in a ConfigMap labeled <code>grafana_dashboard: \"1\"<\/code>, delivered from git; the sidecar loads it. A clicked dashboard lives only in Grafana's database: unreviewable, unversioned, and gone with the pod unless persistence is configured.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.3#explain-red-and-use-and-which-you-would-use-for-", "sec": "4.3", "q": "Explain RED and USE, and which you would use for a queue worker.", "a": "<p>RED = Rate, Errors, Duration, for request-driven services. USE = Utilization, Saturation, Errors, for resources. A queue worker is best served by both plus queue depth: USE for the worker pool, RED-ish for message processing, and saturation is really \"is the backlog growing\".<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.3#loki-returns-nothing-for-namespace-team-a-two-ho", "sec": "4.3", "q": "Loki returns nothing for <code>{namespace=\"team-a\"}<\/code>. Two hops to check, in order.", "a": "<p>The shipper first (Alloy running, not erroring on push, actually tailing that namespace), then Loki (ingester healthy, retention not eating the window, right tenant/org headers if multi-tenancy is on). A healthy Loki with no shipper looks fine and holds nothing, which is why <code>make validate<\/code> demands streams, not readiness.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.3#why-is-a-per-request-id-label-a-bad-idea-in-loki", "sec": "4.3", "q": "Why is a per-request-ID label a bad idea in Loki?", "a": "<p>Labels define streams, and Loki's index is per stream. A unique label value per request creates a stream per request: index explosion, slow queries, high memory. Put high-cardinality identifiers in the log <em>line<\/em> and filter or parse them at query time; that is what Loki's design is optimized for.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.3#turn-alert-me-when-payments-start-failing-into-a", "sec": "4.3", "q": "Turn \"alert me when payments start failing\" into a LogQL-based rule, conceptually.", "a": "<p>A metric query over logs: <code>sum(rate({namespace=\"payments\"} |= \"payment_failed\" [5m])) &gt; 0.1<\/code>, evaluated by Loki's ruler (or mirrored into Prometheus via a recording pipeline). Same alerting mechanics as 4.2; the only difference is where the series comes from.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.3#a-dashboard-imported-from-a-configmap-shows-data", "sec": "4.3", "q": "A dashboard imported from a ConfigMap shows \"datasource not found\" on every panel. What did the export get wrong, and what is the durable fix?", "a": "<p>The JSON was exported \"for sharing externally\", so datasources are <code>${DS_PROMETHEUS}<\/code> inputs (or reference a uid that does not exist here). Fix by provisioning the datasource with a stable <code>uid<\/code> and exporting with the sharing option off so the uid is literal, or with the Grafana Operator's <code>GrafanaDashboard.spec.datasources[]<\/code> mapping <code>inputName<\/code> to <code>datasourceName<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.3#why-is-a-trace-id-a-bad-loki-label-but-a-good-ca", "sec": "4.3", "q": "Why is a trace ID a bad Loki label but a good candidate for structured metadata, and how do you still search it quickly?", "a": "<p>A label defines a stream; a unique value per request means a stream per request, which blows up the index and chunk count. Structured metadata attaches key-values to individual lines without creating streams and remains filterable with label-filter syntax (<code>| trace_id=\"abc\"<\/code>). Alloy and the OTLP ingest path put high-cardinality attributes there by default.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.3#your-shipper-is-a-promtail-daemonset-from-an-old", "sec": "4.3", "q": "Your shipper is a Promtail DaemonSet from an older tutorial. Why is that a problem in 2026, and what replaces it?", "a": "<p>Promtail reached end-of-life on 2 March 2026: no patches, no security fixes. Grafana Alloy replaces it (<code>discovery.kubernetes<\/code>, <code>loki.source.kubernetes<\/code>, <code>loki.process<\/code>, <code>loki.write<\/code>), and <code>alloy convert --source-format=promtail<\/code> migrates the configuration. An OpenTelemetry Collector with the <code>filelog<\/code> receiver or Fluent Bit are the vendor-neutral alternatives.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#jaeger-shows-hundreds-of-one-span-traces-diagnos", "sec": "4.4", "q": "Jaeger shows hundreds of one-span traces. Diagnosis?", "a": "<p>Context propagation is broken: each service starts a new trace instead of continuing the caller's. Look for a proxy stripping <code>traceparent<\/code>, a missing or mismatched propagator (W3C vs B3), a hand-rolled HTTP client that never injects, or an async boundary nobody instrumented.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#head-based-versus-tail-based-sampling-when-does-", "sec": "4.4", "q": "Head-based versus tail-based sampling: when does the difference bite?", "a": "<p>When the interesting traces are rare. Head sampling at 1% will usually miss the one 5-second request that mattered; tail sampling keeps traces that contain errors or exceed a latency threshold, at the cost of buffering complete traces in the collector (and needing all spans of a trace to reach the same collector instance).<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#where-should-sampling-and-redaction-be-configure", "sec": "4.4", "q": "Where should sampling and redaction be configured, and why there?", "a": "<p>In the collector. It is the platform's control point: one config change applies to every service, whereas SDK-side changes need forty teams to redeploy. This is the same \"interface owned by the platform\" argument as section 3.1, applied to telemetry.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#you-annotated-a-deployment-for-auto-instrumentat", "sec": "4.4", "q": "You annotated a Deployment for auto-instrumentation and nothing changed. Two most likely reasons?", "a": "<p>The annotation is on the Deployment's own metadata rather than <code>spec.template.metadata<\/code>, or the pods have not restarted since (injection happens at admission, at pod creation). Third candidate: the annotation value's <code>&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;name&gt;<\/code> does not match an existing Instrumentation CR.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#how-do-traces-metrics-and-logs-tie-together-in-p", "sec": "4.4", "q": "How do traces, metrics and logs tie together in practice?", "a": "<p>Exemplars link metric buckets to sample trace IDs; logs carry the trace ID as a field so you can jump from a slow span to its exact log lines; and the collector can emit all three from the same pipeline with the same resource attributes (namespace, pod, service). \"Correlated by trace ID and resource attributes\" is the sentence.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#your-tail-sampling-collector-runs-three-replicas", "sec": "4.4", "q": "Your tail-sampling collector runs three replicas behind a Service, and the sampling decisions look random. Why, and what fixes it?", "a": "<p>Spans of one trace are spread across replicas, so no single instance sees the whole trace when <code>decision_wait<\/code> expires. Put a first-tier collector with a <code>loadbalancing<\/code> exporter using <code>routing_key: traceID<\/code> in front (or run the sampling tier as one replica, or a StatefulSet the load balancer can address per pod) so every span of a trace lands on the same instance.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#what-is-jaeger-v2-architecturally-and-where-does", "sec": "4.4", "q": "What is Jaeger v2 architecturally, and where does the old jaeger-agent go?", "a": "<p>A distribution of the OpenTelemetry Collector: one binary, collector-style YAML, roles collector/query/ingester/all-in-one, with Jaeger-specific extensions for storage, the query UI on 16686 and remote sampling. Ingest is plain OTLP on 4317/4318. The agent role is deprecated; run a standard OpenTelemetry Collector as DaemonSet or sidecar where the agent used to be.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#a-pod-has-the-injected-init-container-and-the-ri", "sec": "4.4", "q": "A pod has the injected init container and the right annotation, yet no spans appear. Two collector-side reasons and one network reason?", "a": "<p>The Instrumentation's <code>exporter.endpoint<\/code> uses the wrong port for the protocol the agent speaks (Python is HTTP-only, so it must be 4318; Java defaults to gRPC 4317), or the collector pipeline has no <code>traces<\/code> pipeline wired to that receiver. Network: a default-deny NetworkPolicy in the app namespace blocks egress to the collector's namespace. Read <code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT<\/code> in the pod and test the port from inside it.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#how-does-a-metric-panel-jump-to-a-trace-and-what", "sec": "4.4", "q": "How does a metric panel jump to a trace, and what has to be enabled at each layer?", "a": "<p>Exemplars: the histogram sample carries a <code>trace_id<\/code>. The instrumented service or the <code>spanmetrics<\/code> connector emits them, Prometheus keeps them only with <code>--enable-feature=exemplar-storage<\/code>, and the Grafana datasource maps <code>trace_id<\/code> to a Tempo or Jaeger datasource via <code>exemplarTraceIdDestinations<\/code>. Logs join the same picture when the log line carries the trace ID and Loki's <code>derivedFields<\/code> links it.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.5#name-the-dora-four-and-the-system-that-holds-eac", "sec": "4.5", "q": "Name the DORA four and the system that holds each one's raw data in this lab.", "a": "<p>Deployment frequency: Argo CD sync counters. Lead time: git commit timestamps joined with sync timestamps. Change failure rate: sync phases plus rollout outcomes. Time to restore: alerting data (firing to resolved), or incident records. None of them live in one place, which is itself the point.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.5#why-increase-rather-than-the-raw-counter-for-dep", "sec": "4.5", "q": "Why <code>increase()<\/code> rather than the raw counter for deployment frequency?", "a": "<p>The raw counter is cumulative since the controller started and resets on restart. <code>increase(...[24h])<\/code> gives the count within the window and handles resets. For a daily figure that is the right unit; <code>rate<\/code> would give you a per-second number nobody wants to read.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.5#your-new-servicemonitor-for-argo-cd-produces-no-", "sec": "4.5", "q": "Your new ServiceMonitor for Argo CD produces no target. First two checks?", "a": "<p>The endpoint's <code>port<\/code> against the Service's actual port <em>name<\/em> (<code>http-metrics<\/code>, not <code>metrics<\/code>, not 8082), and the label selector against the Service's real labels. Then, as always, Prometheus's own <code>serviceMonitorSelector<\/code> and namespace selector.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.5#how-would-you-measure-request-to-fulfillment-for", "sec": "4.5", "q": "How would you measure \"request to fulfillment\" for a self-service API?", "a": "<p>Difference between the CR's <code>metadata.creationTimestamp<\/code> and the <code>lastTransitionTime<\/code> of its Ready condition. Aggregate it by exporting that delta as a metric (a small exporter or a recording rule over kube-state-metrics custom resource state), then track its p50/p95 over time.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.5#someone-proposes-measuring-developer-productivit", "sec": "4.5", "q": "Someone proposes measuring developer productivity by lines of code. Reply in one sentence.", "a": "<p>Measure outcomes, not output: DORA's four keys plus platform-effectiveness measures (fulfillment latency, adoption, satisfaction) describe whether the system delivers value, whereas line counts reward volume and are trivially gamed.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.5#name-the-five-dora-metrics-as-the-2024-report-de", "sec": "4.5", "q": "Name the five DORA metrics as the 2024 report defines them, and say which group each belongs to.", "a": "<p>Throughput: change lead time (commit to production), deployment frequency, failed deployment recovery time. Stability: change failure rate and deployment rework rate (unplanned deployments to fix user-facing bugs, added in 2024). Reliability is tracked as operational performance beside them, not as a sixth key, and the 2025 report replaced elite/high/medium/low clusters with team archetypes.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.5#the-white-paper-s-organizational-efficiency-grou", "sec": "4.5", "q": "The white paper's \"organizational efficiency\" group: which three latencies does it name, and how would you get the first one out of this lab?", "a": "<p>Request-to-fulfilment latency for a capability, latency to build and deploy a brand-new service to production, and time for a new user to submit a first code change. The first is <code>metadata.creationTimestamp<\/code> to the <code>Ready<\/code> condition's <code>lastTransitionTime<\/code> on the XR or kro instance, exported through kube-state-metrics <code>CustomResourceStateMetrics<\/code> so you can graph p50 and p95 over time.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.5#which-platform-tool-metric-would-you-alert-on-to", "sec": "4.5", "q": "Which platform-tool metric would you alert on to learn that the platform, not the workloads, is degrading, and what kind of SLO fits it?", "a": "<p>A control-plane SLI: Argo CD sync success ratio from <code>argocd_app_sync_total<\/code>, reconcile error ratio from <code>controller_runtime_reconcile_total{result=\"error\"}<\/code> for Crossplane or kro, or admission webhook latency. Give it an availability SLO (say 99.5% successful reconciles over 30 days) and use the multi-window burn-rate rules from 4.2 rather than a fixed threshold, so a slow bleed and a fast outage both page appropriately.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#every-pod-in-a-namespace-is-running-and-the-app-", "sec": "4.6", "q": "Every pod in a namespace is Running and the app returns nothing. First three moves?", "a": "<p>Make something try (a curl or nslookup from inside the namespace) so there is evidence to read; check endpoints/readiness for the Service; check policy verdicts (Hubble DROPPED) and DNS. \"Running\" says the kubelet is happy, nothing more.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#a-pod-is-crashloopbackoff-and-kubectl-logs-shows", "sec": "4.6", "q": "A pod is CrashLoopBackOff and <code>kubectl logs<\/code> shows nothing useful.", "a": "<p><code>kubectl logs --previous<\/code>: you are reading the current, not-yet-started container. Then <code>describe<\/code> for the exit code and reason (137 with OOMKilled points at memory, 1 with app output points at config), then the previous container's <code>lastState.terminated<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#nothing-can-be-created-cluster-wide-every-apply-", "sec": "4.6", "q": "Nothing can be created cluster-wide; every apply times out with a webhook error. What happened and what is the lever?", "a": "<p>An admission webhook's backend is down and its <code>failurePolicy<\/code> is <code>Fail<\/code>, so the API server refuses writes it cannot validate. Restore the backend, or (as a deliberate emergency step) remove/scope the webhook configuration. That is why <code>failurePolicy<\/code> is a governance decision, not a default to accept blindly (5.2).<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#you-have-four-minutes-left-and-no-theory-that-fi", "sec": "4.6", "q": "You have four minutes left and no theory that fits the evidence. What do you do?", "a": "<p>Go back to step 1 and widen: re-check what is affected and the timeline rather than digging deeper into the current object. Most stuck diagnoses are the result of over-committing to the first plausible layer. On the exam, flag it and move on; a stuck task costs you two easy ones.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#what-proves-an-incident-is-over", "sec": "4.6", "q": "What proves an incident is over?", "a": "<p>The same signal that showed the failure, now succeeding: the failing curl returns 200, the alert resolves, the validation check passes. A pod showing Running proves only that a container started.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#every-write-to-the-cluster-fails-with-etcdserver", "sec": "4.6", "q": "Every write to the cluster fails with <code>etcdserver: mvcc: database space exceeded<\/code> but reads work. What happened and what is the order of operations?", "a": "<p>etcd hit its backend quota (2 GiB by default) and raised the NOSPACE alarm, which turns the store read-only. Compact old revisions, defragment each member, then <code>etcdctl alarm disarm<\/code>; raise <code>--quota-backend-bytes<\/code> if the data is legitimate. Then find the producer (event storms, oversized ConfigMaps or Secrets, a controller writing status in a loop) or it recurs.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#a-container-exits-with-code-143-every-30-seconds", "sec": "4.6", "q": "A container exits with code 143 every 30 seconds and the app logs look healthy. Diagnosis?", "a": "<p>143 is SIGTERM: something is asking it to stop gracefully. On a fixed cadence that is almost always a failing liveness probe (wrong port or path, or a timeout shorter than the app's startup). Read <code>kubectl describe pod<\/code> for <code>Liveness probe failed<\/code> events, and fix the probe or add a startup probe. 137 would have pointed at OOM or a forced kill instead.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#you-roll-a-deployment-back-with-kubectl-rollout-", "sec": "4.6", "q": "You roll a Deployment back with <code>kubectl rollout undo<\/code> and two minutes later it is broken again. Why, and what should you have done?", "a": "<p>A GitOps controller re-applied the desired state from git. Either disable or suspend first (<code>argocd app set x --sync-policy none<\/code>, <code>flux suspend kustomization x<\/code>) and undo as a stopgap, or go straight to the durable fix: revert the commit and let the controller roll back for you. Say in the incident notes that the suspension must be lifted.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#which-kubectl-debug-invocation-gives-you-tcpdump", "sec": "4.6", "q": "Which <code>kubectl debug<\/code> invocation gives you tcpdump on a node without full root, and why does the default node debug pod fail at <code>chroot /host<\/code>?", "a": "<p><code>kubectl debug node/&lt;n&gt; -it --image=&lt;image with tcpdump&gt; --profile=netadmin<\/code> adds NET_ADMIN and NET_RAW. The default <code>general<\/code> profile mounts the host filesystem at <code>/host<\/code> but is not privileged, so operations that need root on the host (<code>chroot<\/code>, reading some <code>/proc<\/code> entries) fail; <code>--profile=sysadmin<\/code> is the privileged one.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#define-developer-once-and-grant-it-in-twelve-nam", "sec": "5.1", "q": "Define \"developer\" once and grant it in twelve namespaces. What objects, and how many?", "a": "<p>One ClusterRole plus twelve RoleBindings (each referencing that ClusterRole, each in its namespace). Not a ClusterRoleBinding: that would grant everywhere and fail the \"and not in the other namespace\" check.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#a-user-can-get-a-pod-by-name-but-kubectl-get-pod", "sec": "5.1", "q": "A user can <code>get<\/code> a pod by name but <code>kubectl get pods<\/code> fails. Why?", "a": "<p><code>list<\/code> is a separate verb from <code>get<\/code>, and a rule with <code>resourceNames<\/code> cannot grant a plain <code>list<\/code>. Add <code>list<\/code> (and <code>watch<\/code> if anything streams), accepting that list exposes every object of that kind in the namespace.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#how-do-you-let-a-tenant-read-pod-logs-but-never-", "sec": "5.1", "q": "How do you let a tenant read pod logs but never exec into a pod?", "a": "<p>Grant <code>get<\/code> on <code>pods/log<\/code> and do not grant <code>create<\/code> on <code>pods/exec<\/code>. Subresources are distinct resource strings, which is what makes this expressible at all.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#you-ship-a-new-crd-how-do-existing-view-users-ge", "sec": "5.1", "q": "You ship a new CRD. How do existing <code>view<\/code> users get read access without editing the built-in role?", "a": "<p>Create a ClusterRole with read verbs on your kind and label it <code>rbac.authorization.k8s.io/aggregate-to-view: \"true\"<\/code>. Aggregation folds it into the built-in role automatically, the standard platform-team move when adding an API.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#sealed-secrets-versus-eso-which-would-you-pick-f", "sec": "5.1", "q": "Sealed Secrets versus ESO: which would you pick for a regulated environment with an existing vault, and why?", "a": "<p>ESO: the vault stays the single source of truth with its own audit and rotation, and git holds only references; nothing secret is ever committed, encrypted or not. Sealed Secrets suits clusters with no external store, at the cost of managing (and backing up) the sealing key yourself.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#is-a-kubernetes-secret-encrypted", "sec": "5.1", "q": "Is a Kubernetes Secret encrypted?", "a": "<p>Not by itself: base64 is encoding. At rest it is only encrypted if the API server is configured with an EncryptionConfiguration (optionally KMS-backed); in transit it is protected by TLS. Access control (who has <code>get secrets<\/code>) is doing most of the actual work.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#a-namespace-admin-with-the-built-in-admin-role-c", "sec": "5.1", "q": "A namespace admin with the built-in <code>admin<\/code> role cannot create a RoleBinding to a custom ClusterRole that grants <code>nodes<\/code> read. Why, and what is the least-privilege fix?", "a": "<p>Escalation prevention: you can only bind permissions you hold, and <code>admin<\/code> does not include cluster-scoped <code>nodes<\/code>. Grant the <code>bind<\/code> verb on <code>clusterroles<\/code> with <code>resourceNames<\/code> naming that one ClusterRole. Granting the node permission itself or <code>cluster-admin<\/code> would also work and would be the wrong answer.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#an-oidc-user-in-group-platform-team-is-bound-via", "sec": "5.1", "q": "An OIDC user in group <code>platform-team<\/code> is bound via a ClusterRoleBinding to that group and still gets Forbidden. First command, likely cause?", "a": "<p><code>kubectl auth whoami<\/code> shows the groups as the API server sees them, prefixed by <code>--oidc-groups-prefix<\/code> (or the <code>claimMappings.groups.prefix<\/code> in the structured config), so the real group is something like <code>oidc:platform-team<\/code>. Bind to that string. The same check catches a username prefix and a wrong groups claim name.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#you-rotate-the-aescbc-key-in-encryptionconfigura", "sec": "5.1", "q": "You rotate the aescbc key in <code>EncryptionConfiguration<\/code>. Order of operations, and what happens if you simply replace the key?", "a": "<p>Add the new key as the first provider while keeping the old key second, restart the API servers, rewrite every Secret so it is re-encrypted (<code>kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -<\/code>), then drop the old key. Replacing the key outright leaves every existing Secret unreadable, which surfaces as API server errors on <code>get secrets<\/code>, not at startup.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#eso-a-database-password-rotates-in-vault-every-h", "sec": "5.1", "q": "ESO: a database password rotates in Vault every hour, the ExternalSecret has <code>refreshInterval: 0<\/code>, and pods keep failing to log in. Two fixes and their trade-offs?", "a": "<p><code>refreshInterval: 0<\/code> means create once and never update, so the Secret holds the first value forever. Set a <code>refreshInterval<\/code> shorter than the rotation period (Periodic policy), and make the workload reload (a restart trigger such as Reloader, or read the file mount rather than env vars). Alternatively, if the value must not change under a running pod, keep <code>CreatedOnce<\/code> plus <code>immutable: true<\/code> and roll the pods deliberately.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#a-require-requests-policy-never-fires-in-a-tenan", "sec": "5.2", "q": "A \"require requests\" policy never fires in a tenant namespace. Why?", "a": "<p>Its LimitRange defaults the requests during <em>mutating<\/em> admission, before the validating webhook runs, so the object Kyverno inspects already complies. Admission order (mutate then validate) is the whole explanation, and it generalises to every defaulting mechanism.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#failurepolicy-fail-versus-ignore-argue-both-then", "sec": "5.2", "q": "<code>failurePolicy: Fail<\/code> versus <code>Ignore<\/code>: argue both, then say what bounds the risk.", "a": "<p>Fail: no unvalidated writes, at the cost of the API server refusing writes when the webhook is down (a cluster-wide outage from a policy pod). Ignore: writes keep flowing, at the cost of a bypass window. Bound it with a tight <code>namespaceSelector<\/code>, low <code>timeoutSeconds<\/code>, exemptions for system namespaces, and running the engine with enough replicas that it is not a single point of failure.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#you-tighten-a-policy-to-deny-what-happens-to-the", "sec": "5.2", "q": "You tighten a policy to Deny. What happens to the workloads that already violate it?", "a": "<p>Nothing: admission is write-time. They keep running until their next write (a rollout, a scale, a controller re-create), and then they fail, possibly at 3am. That is why the Audit → report → fix → Enforce sequence exists, and why you check the reports before flipping the dial.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#which-engine-would-you-pick-to-also-create-a-def", "sec": "5.2", "q": "Which engine would you pick to also <em>create<\/em> a default NetworkPolicy in every new namespace?", "a": "<p>Kyverno: generation is a first-class capability there (generate rules that create and keep resources in sync). Gatekeeper validates and can mutate but does not generate; ValidatingAdmissionPolicy only validates. Matching capability to requirement is the whole question.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#why-might-a-platform-team-prefer-validatingadmis", "sec": "5.2", "q": "Why might a platform team prefer ValidatingAdmissionPolicy for a simple rule?", "a": "<p>No extra controller, no webhook, no availability dependency, no version skew; the API server evaluates CEL in-process. The trade is a narrower feature set (no mutation in older versions, no generation, no reporting), so it suits simple invariants rather than a governance program.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#a-kyverno-validatingpolicy-denies-bare-pods-but-", "sec": "5.2", "q": "A Kyverno ValidatingPolicy denies bare pods but a Deployment with the same violating template is admitted. Field?", "a": "<p><code>spec.autogen.podControllers.controllers<\/code> listing <code>deployments<\/code> (and statefulsets, daemonsets, jobs, cronjobs as needed). Autogen rewrites the Pod rule to match the controller's <code>spec.template<\/code>. Without it the Deployment passes and its pods are rejected one level down at the ReplicaSet, the same indirection as PSS. Note autogen and <code>autogen.validatingAdmissionPolicy<\/code> are mutually exclusive.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#which-two-kyverno-controller-flags-must-be-on-be", "sec": "5.2", "q": "Which two Kyverno controller flags must be on before a <code>PolicyException<\/code> has any effect, and how does the report show an applied exception?", "a": "<p><code>--enablePolicyException=true<\/code> and <code>--exceptionNamespace=&lt;namespace&gt;<\/code> (only exceptions created in that namespace count). When applied, the PolicyReport records the rule as <code>skip<\/code> with the exception's name in the result properties, in both admission and background modes.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#a-gatekeeper-constraint-is-applied-the-violating", "sec": "5.2", "q": "A Gatekeeper Constraint is applied, the violating object is still admitted, and <code>status.totalViolations<\/code> grows. Two likely settings?", "a": "<p><code>spec.enforcementAction: dryrun<\/code> (or <code>warn<\/code>) on the Constraint, or the webhook running with <code>failurePolicy: Ignore<\/code> (Gatekeeper's install default) while the webhook pod is unhealthy or timing out. Audit still records violations in both cases, which is why the count grows without any denial. Set <code>deny<\/code>, and check the webhook's <code>failurePolicy<\/code> and <code>timeoutSeconds<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#you-write-a-validatingadmissionpolicy-with-a-mis", "sec": "5.2", "q": "You write a ValidatingAdmissionPolicy with a misspelt field path. Is it rejected? Where do you find out, and what decides the runtime effect?", "a": "<p>It is accepted; CEL type errors appear in the policy's <code>status.typeChecking<\/code> after creation, not as an apply error. At runtime the bad expression errors, and <code>failurePolicy<\/code> decides: <code>Fail<\/code> denies every matching request (with the binding's <code>validationActions<\/code>), <code>Ignore<\/code> lets them through. Read the status, then fix the path.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.3#name-the-three-profiles-in-one-clause-each", "sec": "5.3", "q": "Name the three profiles in one clause each.", "a": "<p>Privileged: no restrictions. Baseline: blocks known privilege escalations (privileged containers, host namespaces, hostPath, extra capabilities). Restricted: additionally requires non-root, no privilege escalation, all capabilities dropped, and RuntimeDefault seccomp.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.3#a-deployment-is-created-and-no-pods-appear-where", "sec": "5.3", "q": "A Deployment is created and no pods appear. Where is the PSS error?", "a": "<p>On the ReplicaSet: PSS is a pod-level admission decision, so the Deployment controller records the rejection one level down. <code>kubectl describe rs -l …<\/code> or namespace events.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.3#you-want-to-know-who-breaks-if-you-tighten-a-nam", "sec": "5.3", "q": "You want to know who breaks if you tighten a namespace. Command?", "a": "<p><code>kubectl label --dry-run=server --overwrite ns &lt;ns&gt; pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted<\/code>: the server evaluates existing pods and warns for each violator, changing nothing.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.3#why-pin-enforce-version", "sec": "5.3", "q": "Why pin <code>enforce-version<\/code>?", "a": "<p>Because profiles gain checks in new Kubernetes releases: with <code>latest<\/code>, a cluster upgrade can start rejecting pods that were compliant before. Pinning turns that into a deliberate migration you schedule rather than a surprise you discover.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.3#pss-or-kyverno-for-images-must-come-from-our-reg", "sec": "5.3", "q": "PSS or Kyverno for \"images must come from our registry\"?", "a": "<p>Kyverno (or Gatekeeper): PSS covers only the fixed pod-hardening dimension and has no notion of registries. PSS is the floor; the engine is for organization-specific rules, and it is fine, indeed normal, to run both.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.3#a-pod-passes-restricted-admission-and-then-sits-", "sec": "5.3", "q": "A pod passes restricted admission and then sits in <code>CreateContainerConfigError<\/code> with <code>container has runAsNonRoot and image will run as root<\/code>. What happened?", "a": "<p>You set <code>runAsNonRoot: true<\/code>, which satisfies PSS at admission, but the image's default user is root, so the kubelet refuses to start it. Set <code>runAsUser<\/code> (and <code>runAsGroup<\/code>) to a non-zero id, or use an image whose <code>USER<\/code> is non-root. PSS checks the spec; the kubelet checks the image.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.3#how-do-you-make-every-namespace-without-labels-e", "sec": "5.3", "q": "How do you make every namespace without labels enforce baseline and warn on restricted, cluster-wide, and what does a namespace label do to that default?", "a": "<p>An <code>AdmissionConfiguration<\/code> with a <code>PodSecurityConfiguration<\/code> whose <code>defaults<\/code> set <code>enforce: baseline<\/code>, <code>warn: restricted<\/code> (plus versions), passed to the API server with <code>--admission-control-config-file<\/code>. A namespace label for a mode overrides the default for that mode only. On a managed control plane you cannot set this, so the fallback is a policy engine that labels namespaces at creation.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.3#restricted-rejects-a-container-for-seccompprofil", "sec": "5.3", "q": "Restricted rejects a container for <code>seccompProfile<\/code>, yet the kubelet runs with <code>seccompDefault: true<\/code>. Why is that not enough?", "a": "<p>PSS evaluates the pod spec at admission; the kubelet default is applied later, at container creation, and is invisible to the admission controller. Restricted requires <code>seccompProfile.type<\/code> to be <code>RuntimeDefault<\/code> or <code>Localhost<\/code> in the spec, at pod level or on every container. The kubelet flag is still worth setting: it protects pods in namespaces that are not restricted.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.4#which-audit-level-would-you-set-for-secrets-and-", "sec": "5.4", "q": "Which audit level would you set for Secrets, and which for Events? Why?", "a": "<p>Secrets: <code>Metadata<\/code>, never Request or RequestResponse, or you write secret values into a log file. Events: <code>None<\/code>; they are high-volume and low-value, and dropping them keeps the log readable. First-match-wins ordering makes those two rules the top of the file.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.4#find-every-request-a-specific-serviceaccount-was", "sec": "5.4", "q": "Find every request a specific ServiceAccount was denied. What does the query look like?", "a": "<p>Filter on <code>.user.username == \"system:serviceaccount:&lt;ns&gt;:&lt;name&gt;\"<\/code> and either <code>.responseStatus.code == 403<\/code> or <code>.annotations[\"authorization.k8s.io/decision\"] == \"forbid\"<\/code>, then print verb and objectRef. That is the audit-log form of the RBAC debugging you did in 5.1.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.4#an-sbom-and-a-vulnerability-report-what-is-the-d", "sec": "5.4", "q": "An SBOM and a vulnerability report: what is the difference and why keep both?", "a": "<p>The SBOM is an inventory of components; the vulnerability report is that inventory joined against a CVE database at a point in time. Keep both because the inventory stays true while the CVE list changes daily; that is exactly why continuous rescanning (the operator) matters more than a scan at build time alone.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.4#why-is-compliance-as-crds-a-platform-engineering", "sec": "5.4", "q": "Why is \"compliance as CRDs\" a platform-engineering idea rather than a Trivy feature?", "a": "<p>Because it makes compliance data a first-class API object: queryable with kubectl, protected by RBAC, watchable by controllers, graphable in Grafana, and reviewable in git. The alternative, reports as files in a bucket, cannot be joined to anything the platform already does.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.4#your-cluster-s-api-server-will-not-start-after-a", "sec": "5.4", "q": "Your cluster's API server will not start after an audit change. First hypothesis?", "a": "<p>The audit policy file is missing or unparseable at the path given by <code>--audit-policy-file<\/code> (or the volume mount is wrong). The API server refuses to start rather than run unaudited: a safety choice that presents as a dead control plane.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.4#your-audit-policy-puts-the-catch-all-metadata-ru", "sec": "5.4", "q": "Your audit policy puts the catch-all <code>Metadata<\/code> rule first and the Secrets rule after it. What is wrong, and what else should precede the catch-all?", "a": "<p>First match wins, so the Secrets rule never applies and every request lands at Metadata (harmless for Secrets in this case, but the same mistake with a <code>None<\/code> rule after the catch-all means the noise is never dropped, and a <code>RequestResponse<\/code> rule after it never raises RBAC changes). Order: <code>None<\/code> for health URLs, leases and kube-proxy watches; <code>Metadata<\/code> for secrets, configmaps, tokenreviews and <code>pods/exec<\/code>; <code>RequestResponse<\/code> for RBAC and policy groups; then the catch-all.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.4#which-layer-catches-each-a-user-reads-a-secret-a", "sec": "5.4", "q": "Which layer catches each: a user reads a Secret; a process in a container starts <code>/bin/sh<\/code>; an image contains a package with a new CVE; a node's kubelet allows anonymous auth?", "a": "<p>API audit log (Metadata on <code>secrets<\/code>); Falco syscall rules; the Trivy Operator's VulnerabilityReport on rescan (or a pipeline scan at build); kube-bench or the Trivy Operator's InfraAssessmentReport and CIS compliance report. Four different sensors, one compliance story; naming which sees what is the answer to \"design a complete audit trail\".<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.4#what-is-the-difference-between-an-sbom-attestati", "sec": "5.4", "q": "What is the difference between an SBOM attestation and a VEX document, and why keep both?", "a": "<p>The SBOM lists what is inside the artefact; VEX states whether known vulnerabilities in those components actually affect it (not affected, affected, fixed, under investigation) with a justification. Scanners join the SBOM against CVE feeds and then apply VEX to suppress non-applicable findings with a recorded reason, which is auditable in a way a bare <code>.trivyignore<\/code> line is not.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#we-installed-a-service-mesh-so-all-traffic-is-en", "sec": "5.5", "q": "\"We installed a service mesh, so all traffic is encrypted.\" Correct?", "a": "<p>No. The default is PERMISSIVE, which accepts plaintext as well as mTLS so migration can be incremental. You need a PeerAuthentication in STRICT mode (mesh-wide, per namespace, or per workload), and you should prove it by watching a non-mesh client get refused.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#where-does-a-workload-s-mesh-identity-come-from-", "sec": "5.5", "q": "Where does a workload's mesh identity come from, and why does that matter to section 5.1?", "a": "<p>From its ServiceAccount: <code>spiffe://cluster.local/ns/&lt;ns&gt;/sa/&lt;sa&gt;<\/code>. It matters because your RBAC design now doubles as your network authorization design (one identity, two enforcement points), and because sloppy SA reuse silently widens both.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#you-need-to-allow-only-post-to-orders-from-one-s", "sec": "5.5", "q": "You need to allow only POST to <code>/orders<\/code> from one service. What must exist?", "a": "<p>An AuthorizationPolicy with an L7 <code>to.operation<\/code> rule <em>and<\/em> a waypoint proxy for the target, because ztunnel alone enforces L4. Without the waypoint the method/path clauses have nothing to evaluate them.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#networkpolicy-or-authorizationpolicy-when-do-you", "sec": "5.5", "q": "NetworkPolicy or AuthorizationPolicy: when do you use each?", "a": "<p>Both, at different layers. NetworkPolicy is CNI-enforced, identity-by-label, works for all traffic including non-mesh workloads. AuthorizationPolicy is mesh-enforced, identity-by-certificate, survives IP churn and works across clusters. Defense in depth: the CNI keeps the reachable set small, the mesh makes the caller prove who it is.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#give-a-scenario-where-spire-beats-a-mesh", "sec": "5.5", "q": "Give a scenario where SPIRE beats a mesh.", "a": "<p>A workload must authenticate to something outside the cluster (a managed database, a partner API, a legacy service) with short-lived credentials and no static secret. SPIRE issues an SVID through the Workload API socket; the mesh only secures traffic between meshed services. Bonus: SPIFFE IDs federate across trust domains, which is how you extend identity beyond one cluster.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#you-add-to-operation-paths-to-an-existing-ambien", "sec": "5.5", "q": "You add <code>to.operation.paths<\/code> to an existing ambient AuthorizationPolicy that uses <code>selector<\/code>, and now every request to the workload fails. Why?", "a": "<p>A selector-targeted policy is enforced by ztunnel at L4, which cannot evaluate L7 fields; rather than ignore the rule Istio fails safe and treats the policy as DENY. Move the L7 rule to a policy with <code>targetRefs<\/code> to the Service or the waypoint Gateway (which requires a waypoint enrolled with <code>istio.io/use-waypoint<\/code>), and keep an L4 policy for identity checks if you still want ztunnel enforcement.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#a-namespace-has-one-allow-policy-for-service-a-s", "sec": "5.5", "q": "A namespace has one ALLOW policy for service A. Service C, with no policy mentioning it, is suddenly refused by every caller. Explain.", "a": "<p>Deny-by-default switches on per workload as soon as at least one ALLOW policy targets it. If the ALLOW policy has no <code>selector<\/code> or <code>targetRefs<\/code>, it applies to every workload in the namespace, so C is now covered and only A's allowed callers pass. Scope the policy to A with a selector, or add an ALLOW policy for C's callers.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#in-linkerd-you-create-a-server-for-port-8080-and", "sec": "5.5", "q": "In Linkerd you create a <code>Server<\/code> for port 8080 and an <code>AuthorizationPolicy<\/code> allowing one client identity. Readiness probes start failing. Why, and what do you add?", "a": "<p>A Server's <code>accessPolicy<\/code> defaults to <code>deny<\/code>, and the kubelet is not in the mesh, so probes on that port are now unauthenticated traffic with no matching authorization. Add an <code>HTTPRoute<\/code> for the probe paths with an <code>AuthorizationPolicy<\/code> whose <code>requiredAuthenticationRefs<\/code> is a <code>NetworkAuthentication<\/code> covering the node CIDRs (or <code>0.0.0.0/0<\/code>), or set the Server's <code>accessPolicy<\/code> to <code>all-unauthenticated<\/code> for probe ports.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#walk-the-spire-chain-from-a-pod-starts-to-it-hol", "sec": "5.5", "q": "Walk the SPIRE chain from \"a pod starts\" to \"it holds a certificate\": which component does what, and where do selectors come from?", "a": "<p>The agent on that node has already attested itself to the server (<code>k8s_psat<\/code>). The pod connects to the Workload API socket (via the <code>csi.spiffe.io<\/code> volume). The agent's <code>k8s<\/code> workload attestor asks the kubelet which pod owns the calling process and derives selectors (<code>k8s:ns<\/code>, <code>k8s:sa<\/code>, labels). It matches them against registration entries under its own parent ID (created by hand or templated by a ClusterSPIFFEID), asks the server to sign, and streams back an X509-SVID plus the trust bundle, rotating before the 1h TTL expires.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#why-scan-in-the-pipeline-and-in-the-cluster", "sec": "5.6", "q": "Why scan in the pipeline <em>and<\/em> in the cluster?", "a": "<p>The pipeline gate blocks known-bad before publication; it can say nothing about CVEs disclosed after the build. Continuous in-cluster scanning catches those, on images already running. Only-CI leaves you blind to every CVE younger than your last deploy; only-cluster lets bad images ship in the first place.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#why-is-signing-by-digest-not-by-tag-the-point", "sec": "5.6", "q": "Why is signing by digest, not by tag, the point?", "a": "<p>A digest names content; a tag is a mutable pointer. Signing the digest means the signature covers exactly those bytes, and anyone who moves the tag cannot forge it. Verification resolves the tag to a digest before checking, and a good admission policy also rewrites the pod's image to that digest so the runtime cannot drift from what you verified.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#signatures-exist-and-someone-still-deployed-an-u", "sec": "5.6", "q": "Signatures exist and someone still deployed an unsigned image. What was missing?", "a": "<p>Admission verification. Signing without an enforcement point is decoration: nothing checks it at the door. Add an image-verification policy that matches the registry glob and denies on failure, and make sure it matches the reference form the pods actually use.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#kyverno-reports-no-signatures-found-for-an-image", "sec": "5.6", "q": "Kyverno reports \"no signatures found\" for an image you definitely signed. Three candidates?", "a": "<p>Registry name/reachability (Kyverno resolves from inside the cluster, so <code>localhost<\/code> is wrong), scheme (plain http needs the engine's insecure flag), and signature <em>format<\/em> skew (a new-bundle signature where the verifier expects the legacy <code>.sig<\/code> tag). Also check the transparency-log expectation if you signed without uploading to Rekor.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#distinguish-sbom-provenance-and-signature-in-one", "sec": "5.6", "q": "Distinguish SBOM, provenance and signature in one sentence each.", "a": "<p>SBOM: what is inside the artifact. Provenance: how and by whom it was built (a SLSA-style attestation). Signature: who vouches for these exact bytes. Three documents, one trust story; provenance and SBOMs are themselves usually signed attestations.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#keyless-verification-which-two-flags-are-mandato", "sec": "5.6", "q": "Keyless verification: which two flags are mandatory, and what does each pin?", "a": "<p><code>--certificate-identity<\/code> (or <code>--certificate-identity-regexp<\/code>) pins the SAN in the Fulcio certificate: the signer's email or, for CI, the workflow URL such as <code>https://github.com/org/repo/.github/workflows/build.yaml@refs/heads/main<\/code>. <code>--certificate-oidc-issuer<\/code> pins who authenticated that identity, for example <code>https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com<\/code>. Without both, any Fulcio-issued certificate would verify, so cosign refuses to run.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#tekton-chains-signs-the-taskrun-annotation-chain", "sec": "5.6", "q": "Tekton Chains signs the TaskRun (annotation <code>chains.tekton.dev/signed: \"true\"<\/code>) but <code>cosign verify-attestation<\/code> on the image finds nothing. Two configuration causes?", "a": "<p>The Task does not emit type-hinted results (<code>IMAGE_URL<\/code> and <code>IMAGE_DIGEST<\/code>, or <code>*ARTIFACT_OUTPUTS<\/code>), so Chains has no image to attach provenance to; or <code>artifacts.taskrun.storage<\/code> / <code>artifacts.oci.storage<\/code> does not include <code>oci<\/code>, so the attestation went to the <code>tekton<\/code> backend (run annotations) only. Check the results on the run and the <code>chains-config<\/code> ConfigMap, then the controller log.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#what-distinguishes-slsa-build-l2-from-l3-and-whi", "sec": "5.6", "q": "What distinguishes SLSA Build L2 from L3, and which one does a signed provenance from a shared CI runner reach?", "a": "<p>L2 requires a hosted platform to generate and sign the provenance with a key the user cannot access. L3 additionally requires the build to be isolated from other builds and from user-controlled steps so the run cannot influence its own provenance or reach the signing material. A shared runner where jobs can read each other's state or the signing credentials stops at L2; L3 needs ephemeral, isolated builders with the signer outside the job (Tekton Chains signing from its controller rather than inside the Task is the pattern).<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#a-kyverno-imagevalidatingpolicy-fails-on-a-priva", "sec": "5.6", "q": "A Kyverno ImageValidatingPolicy fails on a private-registry image with an authentication error, although the pod's <code>imagePullSecrets<\/code> work. Why?", "a": "<p>IVP does not read the pod's <code>imagePullSecrets<\/code> and the global registry credential helper flag does not apply to it; registry access must be declared in the policy's own <code>spec.credentials.secrets<\/code> (Secrets in Kyverno's namespace) or <code>credentials.providers<\/code> for cloud ambient auth. Kyverno fetches signatures from inside its pod, so network, scheme (<code>allowInsecureRegistry<\/code>) and credentials are all evaluated there.<\/p>"}
];
